1
00:00:00,533 --> 00:00:05,567
THEME SONG: In a not too
distant future, next Sunday, AD,

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,800
there was a guy named Joel, not
too different from you or me.

3
00:00:11,833 --> 00:00:16,167
He worked at Gizmonic
Institute, just another face

4
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:17,933
in a red jump suit.

5
00:00:17,933 --> 00:00:22,800
He did a good job
cleaning up the place,

6
00:00:22,833 --> 00:00:25,800
but his bosses didn't like him,
so they shot him into space.

7
00:00:28,733 --> 00:00:34,033
We'll send him cheesy movies,
the worst we can find.

8
00:00:34,067 --> 00:00:35,600
La, la, la.

9
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:37,933
He'll have to sit
and watch them all,

10
00:00:37,967 --> 00:00:40,933
and we'll monitor his mind.

11
00:00:40,967 --> 00:00:43,800
Now, keep in mind,
Joel can't control

12
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:47,833
where the movies begin
or end, because he

13
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:52,667
used those special parts
to make his robot friends.

14
00:00:55,767 --> 00:00:57,233
Robot roll call.

15
00:00:57,267 --> 00:00:57,833
Cambot.

16
00:00:57,867 --> 00:00:58,800
Pan Left.

17
00:00:58,833 --> 00:00:59,200
Gypsy.

18
00:00:59,233 --> 00:01:00,067
Hi, girl.

19
00:01:00,067 --> 00:01:00,800
Tom Servo.

20
00:01:00,833 --> 00:01:02,167
What a cool guy.

21
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:02,700
Crow!

22
00:01:02,733 --> 00:01:03,667
He's a wisecracker.

23
00:01:03,700 --> 00:01:05,700
If you're wondering how
he eats and breathes

24
00:01:05,733 --> 00:01:10,233
and other science
facts-- la, la, la-- then

25
00:01:10,267 --> 00:01:12,800
repeat to yourself,
it's just a show.

26
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:15,133
I should really just relax.

27
00:01:15,267 --> 00:01:21,000
For Mystery Science
Theater 3000.

28
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:32,233
-No.

29
00:01:32,267 --> 00:01:34,367
No, to me, you guys, it's more
like a moment of pure epiphany,

30
00:01:34,400 --> 00:01:36,300
you know?

31
00:01:36,333 --> 00:01:37,033
Outside.

32
00:01:37,067 --> 00:01:37,833
Oh, hi.

33
00:01:37,867 --> 00:01:39,433
Welcome to the
Satellite of Love.

34
00:01:39,467 --> 00:01:41,300
We'll be right with you, OK?

35
00:01:41,333 --> 00:01:43,033
-Rave on, Joel Robinson.

36
00:01:43,067 --> 00:01:44,433
-Rave on, my holy fool.

37
00:01:44,467 --> 00:01:46,400
-All right, dig this.

38
00:01:46,433 --> 00:01:47,367
[Robots scatting]

39
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:50,700
-Once there were big
billows of pure spirit.

40
00:01:50,733 --> 00:01:53,067
I-- we'd run through
it like a sprinkler.

41
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:56,500
I'd try to send it
down baffles and tubes.

42
00:01:56,533 --> 00:02:00,233
Through accident and
experiment, I worked the smoke,

43
00:02:00,267 --> 00:02:04,800
keeping it at my bedside like
a humidifier, a steady stream.

44
00:02:04,833 --> 00:02:06,900
Some nights, like a
dream, I'd see it sputter.

45
00:02:06,933 --> 00:02:09,400
The thing is just a curl now.

46
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:13,900
Time to get on my shoes and
find that billow of pure spirit.

47
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:16,933
I traded away my smoke
tools months ago.

48
00:02:16,967 --> 00:02:20,800
Who needs them when you've
got that steady stream?

49
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:22,500
VOICE (ON LOUDSPEAKER):
Commercial sign in 15 seconds.

50
00:02:22,533 --> 00:02:25,067
-All right, we're
almost done here.

51
00:02:25,100 --> 00:02:27,700
The little stream by
my bed is at its end.

52
00:02:27,733 --> 00:02:30,933
I may not even recognize
the big bale in the cold.

53
00:02:30,967 --> 00:02:34,033
But what good's life
without some pure spirit.

54
00:02:34,067 --> 00:02:35,300
VOICE (ON LOUDSPEAKER):
Commercial sign

55
00:02:35,333 --> 00:02:38,333
in five, four, three, two.

56
00:02:38,367 --> 00:02:39,833
Commercial sign off.

57
00:02:39,867 --> 00:02:40,667
-Yes, yes.

58
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:41,467
Psycho-cali-funka-delic-expiala-
exy!

59
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:55,067
-No, I'm pretty sure, you guys,
I-- it's not supposed to rhyme.

60
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:55,933
It doesn't have to.

61
00:02:55,967 --> 00:02:56,567
-Oh.

62
00:02:56,600 --> 00:02:57,900
-Oh, kind of like a haiku.

63
00:02:57,933 --> 00:02:58,367
Mm-hmm.

64
00:02:58,400 --> 00:02:59,067
Go ahead.

65
00:02:59,100 --> 00:03:00,133
You want to do your thing now?

66
00:03:00,167 --> 00:03:01,833
-Yeah.

67
00:03:01,867 --> 00:03:03,867
My name is Crow.

68
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:04,933
I'm a Pisces.

69
00:03:04,933 --> 00:03:07,133
And I like a girl who's quiet.

70
00:03:07,167 --> 00:03:08,433
Float, floata.

71
00:03:08,467 --> 00:03:11,467
-I want to-- uh,
do you mind if I--

72
00:03:11,500 --> 00:03:11,967
-Oh.

73
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:12,467
Yeah, that's fine.

74
00:03:12,500 --> 00:03:13,833
That's-- I'm done.

75
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:19,333
-Well, hello, Sir
Goofus Von Drake-snot.

76
00:03:19,367 --> 00:03:21,333
How's the poetry enclave going?

77
00:03:21,367 --> 00:03:23,300
Still trying to teach those
little bots about emotions,

78
00:03:23,333 --> 00:03:24,100
huh?

79
00:03:24,133 --> 00:03:25,167
Well, you caught
us at a bad time.

80
00:03:25,200 --> 00:03:27,100
I'm right in the middle
of finishing the elevator,

81
00:03:27,133 --> 00:03:30,200
and Frank's getting
ready for his hat party.

82
00:03:30,233 --> 00:03:32,267
-Mine's going to be
the grandest of all.

83
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,467
-Yep.

84
00:03:33,500 --> 00:03:35,867
Hey, it there's one thing I've
told you about those bots,

85
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:37,600
only hate and snap
judgment are going

86
00:03:37,633 --> 00:03:38,833
to guide them
through the future.

87
00:03:38,833 --> 00:03:41,600
-Uh, Dr. Forrester, you want
to come over here a second

88
00:03:41,633 --> 00:03:44,267
and-- oh, what's this for?

89
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:44,900
-No!

90
00:03:50,167 --> 00:03:50,533
-Oh.

91
00:03:50,567 --> 00:03:51,600
-Oh, man.

92
00:03:51,633 --> 00:03:53,100
They crushed you, didn't they?

93
00:03:53,133 --> 00:03:55,567
It's probably all hot
and it hurts and stuff.

94
00:03:55,600 --> 00:03:58,600
Does this mean we get to do
the invention exchange first?

95
00:03:58,633 --> 00:04:02,633
-No, you don't get to do the
invention exchange first.

96
00:04:02,667 --> 00:04:05,567
Frank, go and get that
unicycle with the monster truck

97
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:06,567
tire on it.

98
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:07,667
-Oh, you mean the one
used for crushing mimes,

99
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:08,567
your naughtiness?

100
00:04:08,600 --> 00:04:10,933
-Yes, the one used
for crushing mimes.

101
00:04:10,967 --> 00:04:11,967
Frank, Frank!

102
00:04:12,033 --> 00:04:13,433
Don't let me fall.

103
00:04:13,467 --> 00:04:14,667
You make me fall,
I'm going to shove

104
00:04:14,700 --> 00:04:17,433
bees in your nose and
bugs down your shirt.

105
00:04:17,467 --> 00:04:21,300
-Would it help to
say, I'm sorry?

106
00:04:21,333 --> 00:04:24,400
-Frank, look at me.

107
00:04:24,433 --> 00:04:27,200
OK, I'm the invention
exchange this week.

108
00:04:27,233 --> 00:04:28,433
Are you happy now?

109
00:04:28,467 --> 00:04:31,067
All right, everybody gather
around and take a big long gawk

110
00:04:31,100 --> 00:04:32,433
at the pocket scientist.

111
00:04:32,467 --> 00:04:34,500
[Laughing]

112
00:04:34,533 --> 00:04:35,000
-Wow.

113
00:04:35,033 --> 00:04:36,067
[INAUDIBLE], man.

114
00:04:36,100 --> 00:04:37,033
Oh.

115
00:04:37,067 --> 00:04:39,267
Hey, nice improvisation
on a standard theme.

116
00:04:39,300 --> 00:04:40,033
Joel?

117
00:04:40,067 --> 00:04:41,033
-Thanks, Carl.

118
00:04:41,067 --> 00:04:43,200
Well, this week's
invention exchange for me

119
00:04:43,233 --> 00:04:45,467
today is of really
an organic nature.

120
00:04:45,533 --> 00:04:48,400
I kind of created it
accidentally, really.

121
00:04:48,433 --> 00:04:51,533
But when you've been marooned
in space as long as I have,

122
00:04:51,567 --> 00:04:54,267
some of your personal effects
can get to be a bit randy.

123
00:04:54,300 --> 00:04:59,333
So I came up with this, the
incredibly stinky sweat socks.

124
00:04:59,367 --> 00:05:00,267
[Groaning]

125
00:05:00,367 --> 00:05:01,233
-Works every time.

126
00:05:05,833 --> 00:05:06,667
-Oh, you got it.

127
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:07,533
That's it.

128
00:05:07,567 --> 00:05:09,200
All right, OK.

129
00:05:09,233 --> 00:05:09,767
Put me down.

130
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:11,333
Get off of me.

131
00:05:11,367 --> 00:05:14,667
Jerry, Jerry, lose
the apron, all right?

132
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:16,800
And as for you, you
barnacle built for two,

133
00:05:16,833 --> 00:05:19,433
you're somehow responsible
for my spine getting snapped.

134
00:05:19,467 --> 00:05:21,433
I'm all right, Frank.

135
00:05:21,467 --> 00:05:24,800
And so you're going to
have to pay in pain.

136
00:05:24,833 --> 00:05:26,567
All right, Frank.

137
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:30,500
And your first film, rather
fittingly, is a safety film.

138
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:31,833
And then we're going
to follow that up

139
00:05:31,867 --> 00:05:34,300
with another
Lippert masterpiece.

140
00:05:34,333 --> 00:05:35,133
OK, Frank.

141
00:05:35,167 --> 00:05:36,700
I'm fine, all right?

142
00:05:36,733 --> 00:05:38,633
And you, mow it, you taco-head.

143
00:05:38,667 --> 00:05:39,500
Come on, get out of here.

144
00:05:39,533 --> 00:05:40,033
Come on.

145
00:05:40,067 --> 00:05:42,533
Hey, hey, hey, hey!

146
00:05:42,567 --> 00:05:43,733
[Alarm]

147
00:05:43,767 --> 00:05:45,267
-Oh, we got movie sign.

148
00:05:58,833 --> 00:06:00,733
-Hey, you don't have those
stinky socks with you?

149
00:06:00,767 --> 00:06:02,767
-Now I know who
the stinky guy is.

150
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,600
-Hey.

151
00:06:04,633 --> 00:06:05,667
-Ha, "Marks the Spot."

152
00:06:05,700 --> 00:06:07,400
Is that, like, "Max, the Knife?"

153
00:06:07,433 --> 00:06:12,667
-No, I think it's about a
dog who changed his name.

154
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,967
-Look, down there,
Raymond Carver.

155
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:16,900
This must be a short.

156
00:06:16,933 --> 00:06:20,233
-And Webber the grill magnate.

157
00:06:20,267 --> 00:06:22,433
-Little bit of a
stretch there, I'd say.

158
00:06:22,467 --> 00:06:24,333
-Me?

159
00:06:24,367 --> 00:06:25,167
-Yeah.

160
00:06:25,200 --> 00:06:27,033
-And George Matthews
as The Beaver.

161
00:06:27,067 --> 00:06:28,667
-You do that every movie.

162
00:06:28,700 --> 00:06:30,333
-It's-- well, you know.

163
00:06:30,367 --> 00:06:31,600
-Yeah, it's neat.

164
00:06:31,633 --> 00:06:33,000
-It's in the contract.

165
00:06:33,033 --> 00:06:33,733
-It's credits.

166
00:06:33,767 --> 00:06:34,400
You gotta do that.

167
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:35,733
-And who's the commissioner?

168
00:06:35,767 --> 00:06:37,367
-Arthur W. Magee.

169
00:06:37,400 --> 00:06:38,767
-Arthur Magee and Molly.

170
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:39,400
-He-he-he.

171
00:06:41,833 --> 00:06:44,667
--and these fabled
traffic accidents--

172
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:45,733
-Mm-hmm.

173
00:06:45,767 --> 00:06:48,900
--involving key workers
in New Jersey war plants--

174
00:06:48,933 --> 00:06:50,433
-When did you start
having these feelings

175
00:06:50,467 --> 00:06:51,500
that you were a commissioner?

176
00:06:51,533 --> 00:06:54,967
--makes it imperative that
we open a second front.

177
00:06:55,000 --> 00:06:56,500
-God, I love you.

178
00:06:56,533 --> 00:06:58,933
I want to shout it
from my oxygen tent.

179
00:06:58,933 --> 00:07:00,667
-Is this for immediately
release, Commissioner?

180
00:07:00,700 --> 00:07:03,433
-No, sit on it for a while.

181
00:07:03,467 --> 00:07:06,667
-The loss of life or
any disabling injury

182
00:07:06,700 --> 00:07:11,467
to a war worker means a definite
setback to our war program.

183
00:07:11,500 --> 00:07:15,467
-If you kill yourselves here,
we can't kill them over there.

184
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,167
-We kill, we maimed--

185
00:07:16,200 --> 00:07:17,033
-Sounds like Commissioner Fudd.

186
00:07:17,067 --> 00:07:20,567
--our fellow countrymen
without malice,

187
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:23,633
without hatred,
without thinking.

188
00:07:23,667 --> 00:07:24,700
-Without energy.

189
00:07:24,733 --> 00:07:25,400
Would you wake up?

190
00:07:25,433 --> 00:07:26,900
Come on.

191
00:07:26,933 --> 00:07:28,933
--to conserve the
lives of the people.

192
00:07:28,967 --> 00:07:29,800
-Mm-hmm.

193
00:07:29,833 --> 00:07:31,733
-And to improve
traffic conditions.

194
00:07:31,767 --> 00:07:33,133
-Im-pwove twaffic conditions.

195
00:07:33,167 --> 00:07:34,900
-Imagine, if you
will, a New Jersey

196
00:07:34,933 --> 00:07:37,000
city with a population
of over 20,000.

197
00:07:37,033 --> 00:07:38,267
-I can almost see it.

198
00:07:38,300 --> 00:07:40,567
COMMISSIONER (OFFSCREEN): Enough
people to fill a large stadium.

199
00:07:40,600 --> 00:07:41,600
[Cheering]

200
00:07:41,633 --> 00:07:42,033
-Free bird!

201
00:07:45,500 --> 00:07:48,033
COMMISSIONER (OFFSCREEN): --are
to suddenly removed by death

202
00:07:48,067 --> 00:07:48,967
or injury--

203
00:07:49,000 --> 00:07:50,967
-Wow, looks like a
Gino Vannelli concert.

204
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:51,867
-Who?

205
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:54,433
--some idea of the
annual traffic toll

206
00:07:54,467 --> 00:07:55,833
in the state of
New Jersey alone.

207
00:07:55,867 --> 00:07:56,767
-Wow.

208
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:59,167
You can't see it from
here, but my torso

209
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:01,100
was fused to a block of granite.

210
00:08:01,133 --> 00:08:05,033
-But most of the drivers and
pedestrians who were involved

211
00:08:05,067 --> 00:08:06,567
didn't break all the rules.

212
00:08:06,600 --> 00:08:07,800
-Just some.

213
00:08:07,833 --> 00:08:10,200
-Just once in a while, they
were careless or inconsiderate--

214
00:08:10,233 --> 00:08:12,533
-Or they didn't want to
live in New Jersey anymore.

215
00:08:12,567 --> 00:08:14,033
-Which is-- oh?

216
00:08:14,067 --> 00:08:16,800
NARRATOR: Yes, driving and
walking in modern traffic

217
00:08:16,833 --> 00:08:19,067
is often careless,
inconsiderate,

218
00:08:19,100 --> 00:08:20,500
and from the automobile
point of view--

219
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:22,167
-But I get a kick out of it.

220
00:08:22,200 --> 00:08:23,933
NARRATOR: --sometimes fantastic.

221
00:08:23,967 --> 00:08:24,900
-It's fabulous!

222
00:08:24,933 --> 00:08:27,900
NARRATOR: --traffic is
a bit fantastic too.

223
00:08:27,933 --> 00:08:28,833
It's about a car.

224
00:08:28,867 --> 00:08:29,767
-Bing!

225
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:30,967
-It's called parallel
universe parking.

226
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:33,000
NARRATOR: --the kind you need
these days to get home in.

227
00:08:33,033 --> 00:08:35,767
And it must be a safe car.

228
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:36,367
One that--

229
00:08:36,400 --> 00:08:37,600
-This one checks out.

230
00:08:37,633 --> 00:08:40,567
NARRATOR: And then there's a
driver, a guy named Joe Doakes.

231
00:08:40,600 --> 00:08:41,733
-Hi, how you doing.

232
00:08:41,767 --> 00:08:42,800
I'm Joe.

233
00:08:42,833 --> 00:08:43,700
See ya.

234
00:08:43,733 --> 00:08:45,000
NARRATOR: Yeah, he
does look familiar.

235
00:08:45,033 --> 00:08:47,067
Sure, he lives on your street.

236
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:50,667
-In fact, he's under
your bed right now.

237
00:08:50,700 --> 00:08:51,067
-Oh!

238
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:51,967
-Oh!

239
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:53,167
NARRATOR: Sorry.

240
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:54,767
Don't get sore, Joe.

241
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:58,100
Just absentminded--
left his car in gear.

242
00:08:58,133 --> 00:08:59,067
-Let it go.

243
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:00,267
-Oh!

244
00:09:00,367 --> 00:09:01,333
NARRATOR: Well,
what do you know?

245
00:09:01,367 --> 00:09:03,100
-He uses the Braille system.

246
00:09:03,133 --> 00:09:04,000
NARRATOR: That a boy, Joe.

247
00:09:04,033 --> 00:09:04,900
Count 10.

248
00:09:04,933 --> 00:09:07,000
Maybe Joe will be
different this time.

249
00:09:07,033 --> 00:09:07,933
-Oh!

250
00:09:07,967 --> 00:09:09,167
-Oh, this is scary.

251
00:09:09,200 --> 00:09:10,900
NARRATOR: There he goes again,
pulling away from the curb--

252
00:09:10,933 --> 00:09:11,333
-Thank you.

253
00:09:11,367 --> 00:09:12,733
Have a nice day.

254
00:09:12,733 --> 00:09:13,700
NARRATOR: Well,
as we were saying,

255
00:09:13,733 --> 00:09:16,933
Joe didn't have much
patience in traffic.

256
00:09:16,967 --> 00:09:18,000
Joe used to say--

257
00:09:18,033 --> 00:09:19,767
-If you're going to bury
a body in a shallow grave,

258
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:21,100
make sure you use Quickline.

259
00:09:21,133 --> 00:09:22,033
Heh, heh!

260
00:09:22,067 --> 00:09:22,967
Well, he used to say that.

261
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:24,767
NARRATOR: Now take a
situation like this.

262
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:26,967
If Joe were in a
hurry, it seemed

263
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:31,800
there was always somebody in
his way, some inconsiderate guy.

264
00:09:31,833 --> 00:09:32,867
-Yep.

265
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:34,000
NARRATOR: Well, if he can
get around him and beat

266
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:34,800
that light at the next corner.

267
00:09:34,833 --> 00:09:35,933
-Hey, buddy.
What are you doing?

268
00:09:35,967 --> 00:09:36,500
-Hot dog!

269
00:09:36,533 --> 00:09:38,867
I got a date with death.

270
00:09:38,900 --> 00:09:40,733
NARRATOR: There goes the light.

271
00:09:40,767 --> 00:09:41,400
-Whoa!

272
00:09:41,433 --> 00:09:43,000
NARRATOR: You see how it is?

273
00:09:43,033 --> 00:09:45,133
As Joe often said--

274
00:09:45,167 --> 00:09:47,767
-Oh, I've enjoyed this
good-natured ribbing.

275
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:48,433
-Thank you!

276
00:09:48,467 --> 00:09:49,400
Thank you very much.

277
00:09:49,433 --> 00:09:50,767
Thanks for almost killing me.

278
00:09:50,800 --> 00:09:51,733
-Yeah, you--

279
00:09:51,767 --> 00:09:52,300
-That's it.

280
00:09:52,333 --> 00:09:52,967
Take me to 3rd Street.

281
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:53,767
NARRATOR: Ah, look at that.

282
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:55,067
As Joe says,
traffic these days--

283
00:09:55,100 --> 00:09:55,933
-Thank you.

284
00:09:55,967 --> 00:09:57,900
NARRATOR: --would
drive a guy nuts.

285
00:09:57,933 --> 00:09:58,600
[Groaning]

286
00:09:58,633 --> 00:09:59,800
NARRATOR: How do you like that?

287
00:09:59,833 --> 00:10:01,100
Didn't even close the door.

288
00:10:01,133 --> 00:10:02,433
[Honking]

289
00:10:02,467 --> 00:10:04,667
-Hey, it's crazy Guggenheim!

290
00:10:04,700 --> 00:10:06,933
NARRATOR: Well, let's leave
Joe to straighten this one out

291
00:10:06,967 --> 00:10:08,767
while we get on with the story.

292
00:10:08,800 --> 00:10:10,700
Now, there was a street
intersection not far

293
00:10:10,733 --> 00:10:11,367
from where Joe lived.

294
00:10:11,400 --> 00:10:12,067
-We call it Blood Alley.

295
00:10:12,100 --> 00:10:13,267
NARRATOR: You pass it every day.

296
00:10:13,300 --> 00:10:17,100
No signals or stop signs, not
much traffic, one of those

297
00:10:17,133 --> 00:10:19,267
places where nobody
bothered to slow down--

298
00:10:19,333 --> 00:10:20,800
a good place for an accident.

299
00:10:20,833 --> 00:10:21,867
-Makes you feel happy.

300
00:10:21,900 --> 00:10:23,933
NARRATOR: Here comes a car
now from one direction.

301
00:10:23,967 --> 00:10:24,500
-What?

302
00:10:24,533 --> 00:10:25,200
NARRATOR: Uh-oh.

303
00:10:25,233 --> 00:10:26,800
Here comes Joe from the other.

304
00:10:26,833 --> 00:10:28,733
-Let's watch the fun.

305
00:10:28,767 --> 00:10:31,067
We think the joke
will be on Joe!

306
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:34,267
[Tires squealing]

307
00:10:34,300 --> 00:10:35,933
-I guess he can't be a witness.

308
00:10:38,433 --> 00:10:40,167
[Interposing voices]

309
00:10:40,200 --> 00:10:41,200
--tragic accident--

310
00:10:41,233 --> 00:10:43,133
--there's a sliver over there--

311
00:10:43,167 --> 00:10:45,267
-(IRISH ACCENT)
All right, folks,

312
00:10:45,300 --> 00:10:46,733
there's a two drink minimum now.

313
00:10:46,767 --> 00:10:48,367
NARRATOR: He's Joe Doakes,
a neighbor of mine.

314
00:10:48,400 --> 00:10:50,400
He's got a lovely wife and
two beautiful children.

315
00:10:50,433 --> 00:10:51,400
-Now I make my move.

316
00:10:51,433 --> 00:10:52,867
NARRATOR: Seemed like
a swell guy, Joe did.

317
00:10:52,933 --> 00:10:56,333
-Well, except when he ate.

318
00:10:56,367 --> 00:10:59,433
-Shows over-- or maybe not.

319
00:10:59,467 --> 00:11:01,267
-Joe?

320
00:11:01,300 --> 00:11:02,833
Crypto-Joe.

321
00:11:02,867 --> 00:11:04,267
-It wasn't my fault.

322
00:11:04,300 --> 00:11:06,000
I had the right of way.

323
00:11:06,033 --> 00:11:08,800
-Ah, tell it to the corpse, pal.

324
00:11:08,833 --> 00:11:11,200
-I was only doing
15 miles an hour.

325
00:11:11,233 --> 00:11:13,033
Hey, where is that guy?

326
00:11:13,067 --> 00:11:14,533
-Uh, he's with your wife, Joe.

327
00:11:14,567 --> 00:11:16,800
-I've been a careful
driver all my life.

328
00:11:16,833 --> 00:11:17,567
-Yeah.

329
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:18,300
-Ask my wife.

330
00:11:18,333 --> 00:11:19,867
She'll tell you.

331
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,333
I've been driving for
15 years, and I never--

332
00:11:22,367 --> 00:11:23,433
-Hey.

333
00:11:23,467 --> 00:11:25,133
Is that Patrick Swayze's father?

334
00:11:25,167 --> 00:11:26,233
-Mm-hmm.

335
00:11:26,267 --> 00:11:27,600
-Are you George Bailey?

336
00:11:27,633 --> 00:11:28,000
Oh, sorry.

337
00:11:28,033 --> 00:11:28,933
Wrong film.

338
00:11:28,967 --> 00:11:30,367
-No use wasting time
with these mortals.

339
00:11:30,400 --> 00:11:31,300
They can't help you anymore.

340
00:11:31,333 --> 00:11:33,200
-Hey, it's Elton John in hell.

341
00:11:33,233 --> 00:11:34,300
--my neighbors, my friends.

342
00:11:34,333 --> 00:11:35,000
They'll understand.

343
00:11:35,033 --> 00:11:35,700
I know them.

344
00:11:35,733 --> 00:11:37,000
-Yeah, you used to know them.

345
00:11:37,033 --> 00:11:38,233
-Yeah, they're your
pallbearers now.

346
00:11:38,267 --> 00:11:39,967
-Come on.

347
00:11:40,000 --> 00:11:40,400
-Take my hand.

348
00:11:40,433 --> 00:11:42,167
-Upsy-daisy.

349
00:11:42,200 --> 00:11:44,600
You've got some tall
explaining to do.

350
00:11:44,633 --> 00:11:46,533
-Do you think they're going
to talk about how Ugly

351
00:11:46,567 --> 00:11:47,433
that guy is?

352
00:11:47,467 --> 00:11:48,867
-Hm.

353
00:11:48,900 --> 00:11:51,100
-Come on, let's go
talk to Hamlet's dad.

354
00:11:51,133 --> 00:11:52,600
-There's Mr. Jordan.

355
00:11:52,633 --> 00:11:54,233
Hey, now over there,
that's Mick Jagger's cloud.

356
00:11:54,267 --> 00:11:55,367
Stay off of it.

357
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:57,567
-Now, this isn't one of those
high school dreams where

358
00:11:57,600 --> 00:12:00,167
they're going to have him
walk around naked, or--

359
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:01,567
-I don't think so.

360
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:04,533
-Got a class he forgot
about until now.

361
00:12:04,567 --> 00:12:05,500
Huh.

362
00:12:05,533 --> 00:12:07,033
What is this, an ice
rink all of a sudden?

363
00:12:07,067 --> 00:12:07,467
Whoa.

364
00:12:10,400 --> 00:12:11,600
-I'll be with you in a moment.

365
00:12:11,633 --> 00:12:14,300
I'm just sealing some fates.

366
00:12:14,333 --> 00:12:17,967
-Today, in dead people's court.

367
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:21,167
-Uh-- ahem-- uh, excuse me?

368
00:12:21,200 --> 00:12:23,033
Uh, pardon your--

369
00:12:23,067 --> 00:12:23,533
-Mr. God?

370
00:12:23,567 --> 00:12:24,333
-Sir?

371
00:12:24,367 --> 00:12:26,333
-Dear diary, it's
hard being God.

372
00:12:26,367 --> 00:12:26,933
Huh?

373
00:12:26,967 --> 00:12:30,633
Oh, later.

374
00:12:30,667 --> 00:12:34,500
-Oh, where are
your skates, boys?

375
00:12:34,533 --> 00:12:35,367
-Oh, my line.

376
00:12:35,400 --> 00:12:36,533
Sorry-- and my hat.

377
00:12:36,567 --> 00:12:38,633
Excuse me, your hon-- your sir.

378
00:12:38,667 --> 00:12:41,267
-Good morning, sir.

379
00:12:41,300 --> 00:12:43,333
-There are no good
mornings here.

380
00:12:43,367 --> 00:12:44,667
-I'll decide if it's
a good morning or not.

381
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:47,433
-What was your name?

382
00:12:47,467 --> 00:12:48,533
-What was my name?

383
00:12:48,567 --> 00:12:49,233
-Huh?

384
00:12:49,267 --> 00:12:50,767
-You mean, sir, I'm finished?

385
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:51,633
All washed up?

386
00:12:51,667 --> 00:12:52,467
-86'ed?

387
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:54,033
-Well, that depends
on the evidence.

388
00:12:54,067 --> 00:12:55,167
-Hasta luego, Buckwheat?

389
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:55,600
-86 holiday?

390
00:12:55,633 --> 00:12:57,700
-Your name?

391
00:12:57,733 --> 00:12:59,633
JOE (OFFSCREEN):
Joseph Doakes, sir.

392
00:12:59,667 --> 00:13:00,733
-Joseph Doakes.

393
00:13:00,767 --> 00:13:01,433
-Funny name, huh.

394
00:13:03,800 --> 00:13:05,167
-Doakes--

395
00:13:05,233 --> 00:13:06,233
-Doakes, let's see.

396
00:13:06,267 --> 00:13:09,167
There's dickweed, dog,
dumb sh-- ah, here it is.

397
00:13:09,200 --> 00:13:10,400
-Joe Doakes.

398
00:13:10,433 --> 00:13:11,267
-Hm.

399
00:13:11,300 --> 00:13:13,533
-Ah, you were born
in New Jersey, eh?

400
00:13:13,567 --> 00:13:15,167
-I thought I smelled something.

401
00:13:15,200 --> 00:13:16,333
-Your age?

402
00:13:16,367 --> 00:13:18,133
-I'm going to be 36
on my next birthday.

403
00:13:18,167 --> 00:13:18,567
[Chuckling]

404
00:13:18,600 --> 00:13:19,767
-Yeah, right.

405
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:21,600
-Next birthday?

406
00:13:21,633 --> 00:13:24,533
-Boy, God sure has a
crummy office, doesn't he?

407
00:13:24,567 --> 00:13:25,533
-Why, he wasn't due here--

408
00:13:25,567 --> 00:13:27,067
-That's because all the
decorators are in hell.

409
00:13:27,100 --> 00:13:27,933
--for another 20 years.

410
00:13:27,967 --> 00:13:30,233
Fine guardian you
turned out to be.

411
00:13:30,267 --> 00:13:31,500
-Why, if it wasn't
for me, your honor,

412
00:13:31,533 --> 00:13:32,733
he'd have been here years ago.

413
00:13:32,767 --> 00:13:34,600
The worst driver in
New Jersey, and you

414
00:13:34,633 --> 00:13:35,700
expect me to keep
him out of trouble.

415
00:13:35,733 --> 00:13:38,733
-My records show no
convictions against this man--

416
00:13:38,767 --> 00:13:39,667
-Oh, he might go free.

417
00:13:39,733 --> 00:13:42,667
-Yeah, he did a favor
for Sinatra once.

418
00:13:42,700 --> 00:13:44,133
-I've been driving
for 15 years--

419
00:13:44,167 --> 00:13:46,533
-Oh, silence.

420
00:13:46,567 --> 00:13:48,633
This court isn't
interested in your opinion.

421
00:13:48,667 --> 00:13:50,467
-Oh!

422
00:13:50,500 --> 00:13:51,733
-Proceed.

423
00:13:51,767 --> 00:13:54,133
-Don't you see, your honor, that
record of no convictions only

424
00:13:54,167 --> 00:13:55,833
proves how well I've guided him.

425
00:13:55,867 --> 00:13:58,267
-You see, I stopped a
car with my face once.

426
00:13:58,300 --> 00:14:00,733
My forehead's all [INAUDIBLE].

427
00:14:00,767 --> 00:14:02,467
-Well, he wasn't a bad fella.

428
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:03,467
-No.

429
00:14:03,500 --> 00:14:04,333
-Good natured egg.

430
00:14:04,367 --> 00:14:04,867
-Egg?

431
00:14:04,900 --> 00:14:05,567
-Kind to his family.

432
00:14:05,600 --> 00:14:06,267
-Hard boiled.

433
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:07,700
-Very considerate of everyone.

434
00:14:07,733 --> 00:14:08,400
-Poached?

435
00:14:08,433 --> 00:14:08,833
Often.

436
00:14:08,867 --> 00:14:09,800
-You see, judge?

437
00:14:09,833 --> 00:14:11,533
I'm not such a bad guy.

438
00:14:11,567 --> 00:14:14,467
Please let me go
back, just this once?

439
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:15,667
Besides--

440
00:14:15,700 --> 00:14:17,767
-Hey, he's got a gun?

441
00:14:17,800 --> 00:14:20,533
-I got almost a full book
of eight coupons left.

442
00:14:20,567 --> 00:14:22,300
-Well, you should have
presented those immediately

443
00:14:22,333 --> 00:14:24,433
upon ordering.

444
00:14:24,467 --> 00:14:25,967
-Let's get on with the charges.

445
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:26,900
-Visa or Mastercard?

446
00:14:26,933 --> 00:14:27,533
Ha, ha!

447
00:14:27,567 --> 00:14:28,467
Little--

448
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:29,933
-How is he on remembering
to give signals?

449
00:14:33,267 --> 00:14:34,533
-[INAUDIBLE] the body
of the letter, huh?

450
00:14:34,567 --> 00:14:35,500
-On signals?

451
00:14:35,533 --> 00:14:36,733
Well, only fair.

452
00:14:36,767 --> 00:14:38,967
Quite a few black
marks against him.

453
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:42,200
But when it came to sounding
his horn, he was pretty bad.

454
00:14:42,233 --> 00:14:43,867
He was always sneaking
up and scaring

455
00:14:43,900 --> 00:14:45,433
the wits out of pedestrians.

456
00:14:45,433 --> 00:14:46,233
-Don't get me wrong.

457
00:14:46,267 --> 00:14:46,867
I enjoyed that.

458
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:48,000
--about night driving?

459
00:14:48,033 --> 00:14:49,600
-Hm.

460
00:14:49,633 --> 00:14:50,400
-I brought a clip.

461
00:14:50,433 --> 00:14:51,467
I don't think it
needs any set up.

462
00:14:51,500 --> 00:14:52,900
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
--called for more caution

463
00:14:52,933 --> 00:14:53,867
in driving in the daytime.

464
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:56,333
But I couldn't get to
first base with him.

465
00:14:56,367 --> 00:14:57,800
-It was our first date.

466
00:14:57,833 --> 00:14:58,633
What a night.

467
00:14:58,667 --> 00:14:59,567
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
--the glare

468
00:14:59,600 --> 00:15:01,700
of his headlights
did to other drivers.

469
00:15:01,733 --> 00:15:02,967
Hm.

470
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,867
Well, there's a lot of little
things here, your honor, but--

471
00:15:04,900 --> 00:15:06,900
-Well, then what the hell
are you wasting my time for?

472
00:15:06,933 --> 00:15:08,933
--show much consideration
for the other guy.

473
00:15:08,967 --> 00:15:12,467
-Hm, a perfect description
of the average driver.

474
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:15,433
-Joe was all right until
he got out on the highway.

475
00:15:15,467 --> 00:15:17,467
Then he was an entirely
different person.

476
00:15:17,500 --> 00:15:20,500
-He became alarmed by my
modeled face and bumpy skull.

477
00:15:20,533 --> 00:15:22,867
--careless,
impatient, irritable.

478
00:15:22,900 --> 00:15:25,367
I can't say that Joe was out
looking for trouble, but--

479
00:15:25,400 --> 00:15:26,900
-But I did find him
down by the waterfront,

480
00:15:26,933 --> 00:15:27,900
dressed in a Spartan costume.

481
00:15:27,933 --> 00:15:30,333
-Kitty, kitty.

482
00:15:30,367 --> 00:15:31,767
-Don.

483
00:15:31,800 --> 00:15:34,633
-You know, I'm thinking
of growing a goatee.

484
00:15:34,667 --> 00:15:38,833
-That description fitted
someone else I know.

485
00:15:38,867 --> 00:15:40,433
If you know who I mean.

486
00:15:40,467 --> 00:15:41,067
-Hitler?

487
00:15:41,100 --> 00:15:41,567
Uh, no.

488
00:15:41,600 --> 00:15:42,500
He drove a stick.

489
00:15:42,533 --> 00:15:43,200
-Yes, your honor.

490
00:15:43,233 --> 00:15:45,800
I had to learn the hard way too.

491
00:15:45,833 --> 00:15:46,700
-Shut up.

492
00:15:55,433 --> 00:15:57,967
-The toughest job I had
with Joe was his speeding.

493
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:00,500
-Yeah, he was worse than
Corey Feldman or Corey Haim.

494
00:16:00,533 --> 00:16:01,933
-Even if he did keep
within legal speed limits,

495
00:16:01,967 --> 00:16:03,867
he might still be
going too fast for--

496
00:16:03,900 --> 00:16:05,133
-Oh!

497
00:16:05,167 --> 00:16:05,567
Whoa--

498
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:07,633
-Rockford turn.

499
00:16:07,667 --> 00:16:08,867
Backwards Starsky--

500
00:16:08,900 --> 00:16:10,000
-Oh, that was great!

501
00:16:10,033 --> 00:16:12,167
-Momentum was just another
name in the physics book,

502
00:16:12,200 --> 00:16:13,933
as far as Joe was concerned.

503
00:16:13,967 --> 00:16:15,100
-Hey, that was a low blow.

504
00:16:15,133 --> 00:16:16,167
-Judge, if I weren't
a ghost, there

505
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:19,767
were times when I would
have been scared to death.

506
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:22,867
Joe's specialty was a right turn
from the center of the street.

507
00:16:22,900 --> 00:16:25,000
He used to wow
them with that one.

508
00:16:25,033 --> 00:16:26,133
-He was great.

509
00:16:26,133 --> 00:16:26,933
What an artist!

510
00:16:26,967 --> 00:16:27,833
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
--concerned

511
00:16:27,867 --> 00:16:29,767
about the drivers he'd cut off.
-Wow.

512
00:16:29,800 --> 00:16:30,567
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
Or the pedestrians

513
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:31,733
who had the right
of way at the--

514
00:16:31,767 --> 00:16:32,700
--Whoa!

515
00:16:32,733 --> 00:16:33,667
-Wow.

516
00:16:33,700 --> 00:16:36,567
-And then when we'd
approach a stop sign,

517
00:16:36,600 --> 00:16:38,000
he'd never stop dead.

518
00:16:38,033 --> 00:16:38,700
Oh, no.

519
00:16:38,733 --> 00:16:40,667
He'd come down to a slow roll.

520
00:16:40,700 --> 00:16:41,633
-Hey, he feels bad enough.

521
00:16:41,667 --> 00:16:43,067
-Sneak through, and
then give it the gas.

522
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:44,900
-And then give them the bird.

523
00:16:44,933 --> 00:16:45,867
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
However, he

524
00:16:45,900 --> 00:16:48,100
did learn one thing
that seemed to stick.

525
00:16:48,133 --> 00:16:48,967
-Gum.

526
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:49,900
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
One day, when

527
00:16:49,933 --> 00:16:51,167
he was driving in a
residential section,

528
00:16:51,200 --> 00:16:55,700
approaching the playground of
a school, without any warning,

529
00:16:55,733 --> 00:16:58,200
a ball bounced out, directly
in front of the car.

530
00:16:58,233 --> 00:16:59,367
-I'm huge.

531
00:16:59,400 --> 00:17:00,533
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN): And
right behind it came a kid.

532
00:17:00,567 --> 00:17:03,867
The little fella disappeared
behind the hood of a car.

533
00:17:03,900 --> 00:17:04,933
-What?

534
00:17:04,967 --> 00:17:05,633
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
Joe jammed on the brakes,

535
00:17:05,667 --> 00:17:08,200
started to jump out of the car.

536
00:17:08,233 --> 00:17:10,533
Then the little boy ran out with
the ball clutched in his hands.

537
00:17:10,567 --> 00:17:11,633
-With his skull
clutched in his hands.

538
00:17:11,667 --> 00:17:13,533
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
--unaware of the close call.

539
00:17:13,567 --> 00:17:15,233
Joe slumped back into
the driver's seat.

540
00:17:15,267 --> 00:17:17,967
-He said a silent prayer, to
Bongo, the god of gravity.

541
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:19,767
-After that, I never
had any trouble with Joe

542
00:17:19,800 --> 00:17:20,733
around schools.

543
00:17:20,767 --> 00:17:23,000
He'd crawl by at a snail's pace.

544
00:17:23,033 --> 00:17:24,567
-Nursing homes, though.

545
00:17:24,600 --> 00:17:25,700
Whoa!

546
00:17:25,733 --> 00:17:27,800
-You see, Joe had two kids
of his own at that school.

547
00:17:27,833 --> 00:17:29,233
-And if his wife
ever found out--

548
00:17:29,267 --> 00:17:32,133
-They're all that way when
it gets close to home.

549
00:17:32,167 --> 00:17:34,333
A pity they don't
always drive as

550
00:17:34,367 --> 00:17:37,200
though their own
families were in danger.

551
00:17:37,233 --> 00:17:40,067
-You make me sick.

552
00:17:40,100 --> 00:17:42,600
-There was another
bad habit Joe had.

553
00:17:42,633 --> 00:17:44,000
[Crashing]

554
00:17:47,200 --> 00:17:48,367
-Oh wait, you'll
want to hear this.

555
00:17:48,400 --> 00:17:51,167
This is good.

556
00:17:51,200 --> 00:17:52,967
-I love Fibber Magee and Molly.

557
00:17:56,167 --> 00:17:58,367
-And at the halftime,
well, injuries

558
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:01,067
are ahead of fatalities
here in New Jersey.

559
00:18:05,733 --> 00:18:07,967
-I'm so ashamed.

560
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,933
-Gulp.

561
00:18:10,967 --> 00:18:13,267
-Uh-huh.

562
00:18:13,300 --> 00:18:16,300
-Wow, looks like, uh,
Nixon's enemy list.

563
00:18:16,333 --> 00:18:17,400
-Scratch another one.

564
00:18:20,133 --> 00:18:22,100
-Hurts, doesn't it?

565
00:18:22,133 --> 00:18:24,133
-So, go on with
your little story.

566
00:18:24,167 --> 00:18:25,733
Go ahead, puny man.

567
00:18:28,700 --> 00:18:30,200
-As I was saying,
sir, one of Joe's

568
00:18:30,233 --> 00:18:32,833
worst habits with hogging
the center of the road.

569
00:18:32,867 --> 00:18:35,267
If some driver behind
Joe honked his horn,

570
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:38,967
well, Joe would lose his temper
and start to put on speed.

571
00:18:39,033 --> 00:18:41,833
But if Joe was behind
some other driver, well,

572
00:18:41,867 --> 00:18:42,967
that was different.

573
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:44,833
He'd cut out with
a burst of speed,

574
00:18:44,867 --> 00:18:47,333
not knowing what was
behind or in front of him.

575
00:18:47,367 --> 00:18:50,333
Then one day, we were
behind another car,

576
00:18:50,367 --> 00:18:51,767
climbing a steep hill.

577
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:52,767
-See?

578
00:18:52,800 --> 00:18:53,900
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
Joe became impatient.

579
00:18:53,933 --> 00:18:56,300
And then came the truck.

580
00:18:56,333 --> 00:18:58,300
Joe saw the danger.

581
00:18:58,333 --> 00:19:01,900
Oh, he was always quick to
see the danger to himself.

582
00:19:01,933 --> 00:19:02,867
The truck was bearing down.

583
00:19:02,900 --> 00:19:04,033
-Uh-- whoa!

584
00:19:04,067 --> 00:19:05,267
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
Joe cut backside.

585
00:19:05,300 --> 00:19:06,100
-Whoa.

586
00:19:06,133 --> 00:19:06,800
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
He made it.

587
00:19:06,833 --> 00:19:08,367
But the poor guy behind him--

588
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:09,967
-The poor guy was James Dean.

589
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:11,133
The end.

590
00:19:11,167 --> 00:19:13,333
-Sir, even a ghost has feelings.

591
00:19:13,367 --> 00:19:16,000
Why, I was so disgusted
with his selfishness--

592
00:19:16,033 --> 00:19:18,433
-Why, I booed him,
right in his front seat.

593
00:19:18,467 --> 00:19:20,533
-If I hadn't given
you my solemn promise

594
00:19:20,567 --> 00:19:22,467
to stick it out with
him, I would have.

595
00:19:22,500 --> 00:19:23,867
-Just relax, now.

596
00:19:23,900 --> 00:19:24,800
Take it easy.

597
00:19:24,833 --> 00:19:28,200
-Sorry I blew up like
that, your-- um--

598
00:19:28,233 --> 00:19:29,300
-By the way--

599
00:19:29,333 --> 00:19:30,267
-What are you
doing Friday night?

600
00:19:30,300 --> 00:19:34,000
--does he ever drive
when he's been drinking?

601
00:19:34,033 --> 00:19:34,700
-Oh, boy.

602
00:19:34,733 --> 00:19:36,400
I need my other scroll for that.

603
00:19:36,433 --> 00:19:39,500
That's a doozy.

604
00:19:39,533 --> 00:19:41,000
-Well, sir, he--

605
00:19:41,033 --> 00:19:44,800
-Guardian, I want
the whole truth.

606
00:19:44,833 --> 00:19:46,267
-So help me me.

607
00:19:46,333 --> 00:19:49,433
-Well, sir, Joe did
take a nip occasionally.

608
00:19:49,467 --> 00:19:52,200
He wasn't really what
you'd call a drinker.

609
00:19:52,233 --> 00:19:53,600
Just a cocktail now and then.

610
00:19:53,633 --> 00:19:54,533
-Just a half gallon
now and then.

611
00:19:54,567 --> 00:19:57,000
-You know how the
boys are, your honor.

612
00:19:57,033 --> 00:19:58,867
-Yes, I know how the boys are.

613
00:19:58,900 --> 00:19:59,967
-And I know where the boys are.

614
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:03,400
-I have a book full of those
"just a cocktail or two"

615
00:20:03,433 --> 00:20:04,300
drinkers.

616
00:20:04,333 --> 00:20:05,000
-Jerks wouldn't let
me in their club.

617
00:20:05,033 --> 00:20:08,300
-Drivers and pedestrians too.

618
00:20:08,333 --> 00:20:10,067
Dead ones.

619
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:10,967
-Big ones.

620
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:12,967
-Just a cocktail or two.

621
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:14,167
-Phooey!

622
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:18,000
-Any abnormal physical or
mental condition due to drinking

623
00:20:18,033 --> 00:20:18,467
liquor--

624
00:20:18,500 --> 00:20:19,167
-Is monotonous.

625
00:20:19,200 --> 00:20:21,967
--is intoxication in this state.

626
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:25,467
The careless driver or
pedestrian is usually careless

627
00:20:25,500 --> 00:20:28,467
regarding the other
fellow's rights and safety.

628
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:31,233
But as to his own
safety and well being--

629
00:20:31,267 --> 00:20:32,333
-Mm-hmm.

630
00:20:32,367 --> 00:20:34,300
--he's downright selfish.

631
00:20:34,333 --> 00:20:35,533
-Pah!

632
00:20:35,567 --> 00:20:40,100
-Now, dilute that
dangerous frame of mind

633
00:20:40,133 --> 00:20:42,267
with just a cocktail or two--

634
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:42,667
-Oh, OK.

635
00:20:42,700 --> 00:20:43,167
Thanks.

636
00:20:43,200 --> 00:20:44,167
I'll take scotch.

637
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:47,367
--then he even forgets
about his own safety.

638
00:20:47,400 --> 00:20:49,067
That is one of the
reasons why that clock--

639
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:50,500
-Is this going to
be on the final?

640
00:20:50,533 --> 00:20:52,533
--ticked off a traffic
accident in this state--

641
00:20:52,533 --> 00:20:55,333
-Hey, if we're talking mob
murders, we'd be digital.

642
00:20:55,367 --> 00:20:56,433
[Humming a fight song]

643
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:03,300
-Incidentally, was there any
traffic rule he didn't violate?

644
00:21:03,333 --> 00:21:03,833
-Yes, sir.

645
00:21:03,867 --> 00:21:05,233
I never hit and run.

646
00:21:05,267 --> 00:21:07,167
-Oh, well, that
changes everything.

647
00:21:07,200 --> 00:21:09,000
-If you had, you would
have been booked in--

648
00:21:09,033 --> 00:21:09,533
-Ahem.

649
00:21:09,567 --> 00:21:10,600
-In phlegm?

650
00:21:10,633 --> 00:21:13,300
-In a lower court.

651
00:21:13,333 --> 00:21:16,433
So you never hit and run.

652
00:21:16,467 --> 00:21:18,667
Why, that's so
despicable an act--

653
00:21:18,700 --> 00:21:19,667
-Des-peck-a-pick?

654
00:21:19,700 --> 00:21:22,100
--that I wouldn't even
accuse you of doing it.

655
00:21:22,133 --> 00:21:23,567
-Never hit and run?

656
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:24,600
Why, I ought to throw the--

657
00:21:24,633 --> 00:21:27,067
-Was his car
regularly inspected?

658
00:21:27,133 --> 00:21:28,033
-Yes, sir.

659
00:21:28,067 --> 00:21:29,467
-Hey, I'm no Commie.

660
00:21:29,500 --> 00:21:30,667
-Oh, yeah.

661
00:21:30,700 --> 00:21:32,333
At the last minute.

662
00:21:32,367 --> 00:21:35,133
But you'll never know how I
had to nag at his conscience

663
00:21:35,167 --> 00:21:37,267
to get him over
for an inspection.

664
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:40,167
Why, I had to throw all the
statistics in the book at him.

665
00:21:40,200 --> 00:21:44,200
Over 256,000 cars rejected
for punked steering gears.

666
00:21:44,233 --> 00:21:45,333
-Oh, Joe.

667
00:21:45,367 --> 00:21:49,333
-And over 800,000 cars
rejected for dangerous brakes.

668
00:21:49,367 --> 00:21:51,667
And over 1,400,000--

669
00:21:51,700 --> 00:21:53,733
-OK, thank you, Mr. Spock.

670
00:21:53,767 --> 00:21:55,667
-And those figures
always got him.

671
00:21:55,700 --> 00:21:56,500
-Uh-huh.

672
00:21:56,533 --> 00:21:57,433
-Why, here's proof
that I got him

673
00:21:57,467 --> 00:21:59,933
to keep his car in
good running condition.

674
00:21:59,933 --> 00:22:02,500
He can't blame his
accident on his car.

675
00:22:02,533 --> 00:22:04,367
-Well, we'll mark
that exhibit B.

676
00:22:04,400 --> 00:22:06,300
-And I'll slit my wrist
with it, because I'm

677
00:22:06,333 --> 00:22:07,367
bored by this whole thing.

678
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:10,700
-He is exhibit A. I've
heard about enough of him

679
00:22:10,733 --> 00:22:12,467
as a driver.

680
00:22:12,500 --> 00:22:14,767
-Now, how is he as
a cocktail waitress?

681
00:22:14,800 --> 00:22:17,667
-Well, sir, as a pedestrian,
he was different.

682
00:22:17,700 --> 00:22:20,600
As a driver, he thought
he owned the highway,

683
00:22:20,633 --> 00:22:22,633
but as a pedestrian,
he knew he owned it.

684
00:22:22,667 --> 00:22:23,867
-Oh.

685
00:22:23,900 --> 00:22:25,467
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN): He'd weave
through traffic like a mouse

686
00:22:25,500 --> 00:22:26,400
through a maze--

687
00:22:26,433 --> 00:22:27,867
-Squeaking and calling
himself Algernon.

688
00:22:27,900 --> 00:22:29,767
GUARDIAN (OFFSCREEN):
--behind a wheel.

689
00:22:29,800 --> 00:22:31,300
And heaven help the
driver who might

690
00:22:31,333 --> 00:22:33,567
come close enough
to frighten Joe.

691
00:22:33,633 --> 00:22:34,833
-And bless the beasts
and the children.

692
00:22:34,867 --> 00:22:35,767
Oh.

693
00:22:35,800 --> 00:22:36,700
-One day we were at
a busy intersection.

694
00:22:36,733 --> 00:22:39,967
My mind was on other
things for a minute.

695
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:43,200
As the red light flashed
on, Joe got away from me

696
00:22:43,233 --> 00:22:45,300
and started diagonally
across the intersection.

697
00:22:45,333 --> 00:22:46,900
-Doh.

698
00:22:46,933 --> 00:22:49,700
-Now, instead of walking
over to this corner

699
00:22:49,733 --> 00:22:54,400
and then over to this corner,
Joe decides to take a shortcut.

700
00:22:54,433 --> 00:22:57,467
He had, uh, just about
reached this point--

701
00:22:57,500 --> 00:22:59,500
-That's when he
stepped into a wormhole

702
00:22:59,533 --> 00:23:01,467
and was transported
to the 16th century.

703
00:23:04,933 --> 00:23:06,533
-I see.

704
00:23:06,567 --> 00:23:08,767
You tried to get
here earlier, eh?

705
00:23:08,800 --> 00:23:09,933
-Yes, sir.

706
00:23:09,967 --> 00:23:11,700
He almost made it too.

707
00:23:11,733 --> 00:23:14,800
He found out the hypotenuse
of a triangle in traffic

708
00:23:14,833 --> 00:23:16,833
is the shortest road
to the hospital.

709
00:23:16,867 --> 00:23:18,267
-Oh, that was a good one.

710
00:23:18,300 --> 00:23:19,767
You looked like a
hamburger that time.

711
00:23:19,800 --> 00:23:21,433
-Two long, happy months.

712
00:23:21,467 --> 00:23:23,700
My only rest in
the 15 long years

713
00:23:23,733 --> 00:23:25,700
that I had to spend with
that guy's conscience.

714
00:23:25,733 --> 00:23:26,967
-Aw.

715
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:28,800
-(SIGHING) Ah, what happy days.

716
00:23:28,833 --> 00:23:31,967
There I was with not
a care in the world.

717
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:33,200
-Get on with it.

718
00:23:33,233 --> 00:23:35,267
-Just as long as he kept out
of traffic, it was wonderful.

719
00:23:35,300 --> 00:23:37,633
But of course, all good
things come to an end.

720
00:23:37,667 --> 00:23:39,267
-Including the Wildwood weed.

721
00:23:41,800 --> 00:23:43,433
-Well, for the
next few weeks, Joe

722
00:23:43,467 --> 00:23:45,833
was the perfect
pedestrian and driver.

723
00:23:45,867 --> 00:23:49,633
Then one night, he-- he, uh,
stayed up with a sick friend.

724
00:23:49,667 --> 00:23:50,967
-What's that?

725
00:23:51,000 --> 00:23:51,633
-Well, uh--

726
00:23:51,667 --> 00:23:52,833
-Well, he got ploughed, OK?

727
00:23:52,867 --> 00:23:53,900
--he had a cocktail or two.

728
00:23:53,933 --> 00:23:55,167
-Jiminy.

729
00:23:55,200 --> 00:23:57,567
-But maybe it was like he said,
it was the hamburgers he ate.

730
00:23:57,600 --> 00:24:00,100
Anyways, the next morning,
his head throbbed so hard,

731
00:24:00,133 --> 00:24:00,900
I almost felt it.

732
00:24:00,933 --> 00:24:02,133
-He was higher
than Judy Garland.

733
00:24:02,167 --> 00:24:05,100
-And in that condition, he got
behind the wheel of his car.

734
00:24:05,133 --> 00:24:07,567
And that had to be his
last day, your honor.

735
00:24:07,600 --> 00:24:08,967
Ooh, what a pity.

736
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:10,767
He really went to
town or somewhere.

737
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:13,933
-He landed right on the
X. That takes direction.

738
00:24:13,933 --> 00:24:16,433
-From sheer exhaustion,
I closed my eyes

739
00:24:16,467 --> 00:24:19,667
for just a fraction of a second.

740
00:24:19,700 --> 00:24:20,833
I failed you, sir.

741
00:24:20,867 --> 00:24:22,133
-Who's on trial here?

742
00:24:22,167 --> 00:24:25,467
-I was so weary, nothing
seemed to matter.

743
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:27,633
-Oh.

744
00:24:27,667 --> 00:24:28,767
-15 years--

745
00:24:28,800 --> 00:24:29,967
-On a dead man's chest.

746
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,067
--as Joe Doakes'
driving conscience.

747
00:24:32,100 --> 00:24:33,000
-Sorry.

748
00:24:33,033 --> 00:24:37,000
-Is Punishment enough,
even for a ghost.

749
00:24:37,033 --> 00:24:38,900
You have completely
paid the penalty

750
00:24:38,933 --> 00:24:42,367
for your own
carelessness in traffic.

751
00:24:42,400 --> 00:24:43,867
You are now a free ghost.

752
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:45,933
-See the clerk,
and leave the gown.

753
00:24:45,967 --> 00:24:46,833
-Go?

754
00:24:46,833 --> 00:24:47,233
I can go?

755
00:24:47,233 --> 00:24:48,467
-Free ghost!

756
00:24:48,500 --> 00:24:49,433
-Oh!

757
00:24:49,467 --> 00:24:50,200
-"Stairway to Heaven."

758
00:24:50,233 --> 00:24:51,167
-Woo!

759
00:24:51,200 --> 00:24:52,100
-What a relief.

760
00:24:52,133 --> 00:24:52,967
What a relief.

761
00:24:53,000 --> 00:24:54,467
-The hell with Joe.

762
00:24:54,500 --> 00:24:55,000
I'm out of here.

763
00:24:58,033 --> 00:25:01,633
-He was a-- with the--

764
00:25:01,667 --> 00:25:02,567
-It's high noon.

765
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:07,133
[Crashing]

766
00:25:07,167 --> 00:25:08,933
-Oh, that's the angel leaving.

767
00:25:08,967 --> 00:25:10,133
He's dead now.

768
00:25:10,167 --> 00:25:13,133
-Oh, I think that's James Dean
or Isadora Duncan or maybe

769
00:25:13,167 --> 00:25:15,067
Jayne Mansfield.

770
00:25:15,100 --> 00:25:16,067
-Hm.

771
00:25:16,100 --> 00:25:17,633
[Humming a fight song]

772
00:25:21,800 --> 00:25:23,767
-Can I watch some
TV now, grandpa?

773
00:25:26,267 --> 00:25:26,700
-Stop it.

774
00:25:26,733 --> 00:25:27,667
Stop it.
-What?

775
00:25:27,700 --> 00:25:28,233
What am I doing?

776
00:25:28,267 --> 00:25:29,000
What?

777
00:25:29,033 --> 00:25:32,300
-Stop it?

778
00:25:32,333 --> 00:25:35,233
Oh, I wish I could stop it.

779
00:25:35,267 --> 00:25:37,033
-Oh, I wish I were dead.

780
00:25:37,067 --> 00:25:40,267
Oh, I am.

781
00:25:40,300 --> 00:25:44,033
-Joseph Doakes, as to
passing sentence upon you,

782
00:25:44,067 --> 00:25:48,933
I must leave that to this jury
of drivers and pedestrians.

783
00:25:48,967 --> 00:25:49,667
-Oh, guilty.

784
00:25:49,700 --> 00:25:50,100
Hang him.

785
00:25:50,133 --> 00:25:51,000
String him up.

786
00:25:51,033 --> 00:25:52,733
And get the director too.

787
00:25:52,733 --> 00:25:54,700
-Ladies and gentlemen
of the jury--

788
00:25:54,733 --> 00:25:55,100
-Jury?

789
00:25:55,133 --> 00:25:56,000
-Huh?

790
00:25:56,033 --> 00:25:57,933
-Before you retire
to pass judgement--

791
00:25:57,967 --> 00:25:59,233
-Oh, that's great.

792
00:25:59,267 --> 00:26:02,400
We're stranded in space, and we
still have to pull jury duty.

793
00:26:02,433 --> 00:26:03,667
-Not me.

794
00:26:03,700 --> 00:26:04,667
-Hey, this is important.

795
00:26:04,700 --> 00:26:05,533
Pay attention.

796
00:26:05,567 --> 00:26:07,700
-He was kind and
considerate to everyone.

797
00:26:07,733 --> 00:26:09,933
-Except his grandmother
and the kitty.

798
00:26:09,967 --> 00:26:10,800
-OK, all right.

799
00:26:10,833 --> 00:26:11,667
Come on.
-Let's get out of here, come on.

800
00:26:11,700 --> 00:26:12,333
-Come on.

801
00:26:12,367 --> 00:26:13,200
-We're blowing out of here.

802
00:26:13,233 --> 00:26:14,267
You can just hang around.

803
00:26:14,300 --> 00:26:15,267
You can stay, Crow.

804
00:26:15,300 --> 00:26:19,767
-He was inconsiderate on
dimming his headlights.

805
00:26:19,800 --> 00:26:22,300
He wasn't too
careful about speed.

806
00:26:22,333 --> 00:26:22,933
-Mm-mm.

807
00:26:22,967 --> 00:26:23,633
Mm-mm.

808
00:26:23,667 --> 00:26:26,767
-He seldom came to a full stop.

809
00:26:26,833 --> 00:26:27,333
-I remember that.

810
00:26:27,333 --> 00:26:28,267
-At stop signs.

811
00:26:28,300 --> 00:26:29,700
-Yep.

812
00:26:29,733 --> 00:26:32,200
-He frequently hogged the road--

813
00:26:32,233 --> 00:26:32,967
-Hogging the road.
Right.

814
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:33,667
I remember.

815
00:26:33,700 --> 00:26:37,667
--and sometimes passed on hills.

816
00:26:37,700 --> 00:26:40,833
I must say, in
his favor, that he

817
00:26:40,867 --> 00:26:43,067
did have regular
car inspections.

818
00:26:43,100 --> 00:26:44,900
-Yeah.

819
00:26:44,933 --> 00:26:45,833
-And now--

820
00:26:45,867 --> 00:26:47,067
-Ah!

821
00:26:47,100 --> 00:26:53,733
--most importantly, before you
hand in your verdict, think.

822
00:26:53,767 --> 00:26:57,400
Consider carefully
your own qualifications

823
00:26:57,433 --> 00:27:00,067
as a driver and a pedestrian.

824
00:27:00,067 --> 00:27:01,833
-My qualifications?

825
00:27:01,867 --> 00:27:03,533
-Are you qualified--

826
00:27:03,567 --> 00:27:04,900
-Am I qualified?

827
00:27:04,933 --> 00:27:06,500
--to pass judgment on Joe?

828
00:27:06,533 --> 00:27:07,300
-Yeah, I don't know.

829
00:27:07,333 --> 00:27:08,467
I'm not qualified.

830
00:27:23,933 --> 00:27:26,433
-Hm, qualified.

831
00:27:26,467 --> 00:27:28,067
Qualified.

832
00:27:28,100 --> 00:27:30,433
Wow, what a question.

833
00:27:30,467 --> 00:27:31,900
Me, Crow T. Robot.

834
00:27:31,933 --> 00:27:32,733
What do I think?

835
00:27:32,767 --> 00:27:33,733
Am I qualified?

836
00:27:33,767 --> 00:27:35,500
Wow, that's a heavy burden.

837
00:27:35,533 --> 00:27:37,200
How can I make a difference?

838
00:27:37,233 --> 00:27:39,233
Can I make a difference?

839
00:27:39,267 --> 00:27:41,400
Surely I'm but a
single bot, alone

840
00:27:41,433 --> 00:27:44,833
as it were in the
vast universe thing.

841
00:27:44,867 --> 00:27:46,200
Maybe I can't change the world.

842
00:27:46,233 --> 00:27:49,467
Perhaps I've looked at
life from upside down.

843
00:27:49,500 --> 00:27:50,667
Hm.

844
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:55,367
Hey Cambot, move it in a little,
and cue that moody music.

845
00:27:55,400 --> 00:27:58,333
Well, what would Joel do
in a situation like this?

846
00:27:58,367 --> 00:27:58,933
No.

847
00:27:58,967 --> 00:28:00,133
No.

848
00:28:00,167 --> 00:28:03,967
No, no-- I've got to learn
to think for myself, to stand

849
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:05,967
on my own two
foot-like appendages.

850
00:28:06,033 --> 00:28:08,333
Seize the day, yeah.

851
00:28:08,367 --> 00:28:10,133
Think globally, act locally.

852
00:28:10,167 --> 00:28:13,133
Yes, by God, I can do it.

853
00:28:13,167 --> 00:28:16,300
Why, I could start a
letter-writing campaign.

854
00:28:16,333 --> 00:28:17,167
Yeah, that would help.

855
00:28:17,200 --> 00:28:19,600
And uh, I could
organize a bake sale.

856
00:28:19,633 --> 00:28:23,500
Or hey, we could all help.

857
00:28:23,533 --> 00:28:24,567
Come on, friends.

858
00:28:24,600 --> 00:28:25,800
Run to your window and
shout, I'm really cheesed,

859
00:28:25,833 --> 00:28:30,000
and I'm not going to hang around
till this thing gets better.

860
00:28:30,033 --> 00:28:33,067
Why, organize an improv group
and do guerrilla theater

861
00:28:33,100 --> 00:28:35,233
at the food court in your mall.

862
00:28:35,267 --> 00:28:36,533
Dress a little differently.

863
00:28:36,567 --> 00:28:39,033
Make it more exciting
for you and your spouse.

864
00:28:39,067 --> 00:28:41,200
Or here's an idea-- toss
a little Cajun spice

865
00:28:41,233 --> 00:28:44,133
into the party mix
and watch the fun.

866
00:28:44,167 --> 00:28:47,000
Put on a one-man show and talk
about your true inner feelings

867
00:28:47,033 --> 00:28:49,233
in an emotionally-charged,
gut-wrenching, autobiographical

868
00:28:49,267 --> 00:28:50,500
account of your
warped adolescence,

869
00:28:50,533 --> 00:28:52,067
and then watch the
grant money come in.

870
00:28:52,100 --> 00:28:52,633
Woo!

871
00:28:52,667 --> 00:28:53,733
But don't snap judge me.

872
00:28:53,767 --> 00:28:57,233
And then watch that-- I
know, put a drop of vanilla

873
00:28:57,267 --> 00:28:59,500
behind each ear, and you'll
smell like a cookie all day.

874
00:28:59,533 --> 00:29:02,400
Or, uh, eat an apple,
nature's toothbrush.

875
00:29:02,433 --> 00:29:03,633
Ask Mr. Owl how
many licks it takes

876
00:29:03,667 --> 00:29:05,700
to get to the Tootsie center.

877
00:29:05,733 --> 00:29:08,133
Have you met everyone
on your block?

878
00:29:08,167 --> 00:29:10,600
Now would be a nice time
to start, don't you think?

879
00:29:10,633 --> 00:29:11,567
Hm?

880
00:29:11,600 --> 00:29:14,033
In the classroom, slide
your desks together

881
00:29:14,033 --> 00:29:15,567
and create an ecology symbol.

882
00:29:15,600 --> 00:29:18,133
Um, police the lives
of those around you,

883
00:29:18,167 --> 00:29:21,500
and get your sensibilities
way the heck out of whack.

884
00:29:21,533 --> 00:29:23,667
Parade up and down the
street in your underwear.

885
00:29:23,700 --> 00:29:25,233
Impose your ideals on others.

886
00:29:25,267 --> 00:29:26,433
It's easy.

887
00:29:26,467 --> 00:29:28,567
Crush someone with an emotional
word or an enigmatic look.

888
00:29:28,600 --> 00:29:29,467
You decide.

889
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:29,967
You do it.

890
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:31,100
I'm sick of this.

891
00:29:31,133 --> 00:29:32,200
I can't make a decision.

892
00:29:32,233 --> 00:29:34,333
I'm no good at
this sort of thing.

893
00:29:34,367 --> 00:29:35,567
It's up to you.

894
00:29:35,600 --> 00:29:40,433
I'm passing the buck to you,
and I've got commercial sign.

895
00:29:40,467 --> 00:29:44,533
Oh, the pain, the pain.

896
00:29:44,567 --> 00:29:45,167
[Sigh]

897
00:29:54,567 --> 00:29:56,267
-Oh no, Robert Lippert?

898
00:29:56,300 --> 00:29:57,133
Oh, yikes.

899
00:29:57,167 --> 00:29:59,167
-[INAUDIBLE] is
extending us a welcome.

900
00:29:59,200 --> 00:30:01,433
-Bedside potatoes.

901
00:30:01,467 --> 00:30:03,333
-"King Dinosaur."

902
00:30:03,367 --> 00:30:04,667
-Breakfast with the king or--

903
00:30:04,700 --> 00:30:06,067
-I can't do that.

904
00:30:06,100 --> 00:30:06,800
Excuse me.

905
00:30:06,833 --> 00:30:07,500
-You OK, Crow?

906
00:30:07,533 --> 00:30:08,667
-Oh, yeah.

907
00:30:08,700 --> 00:30:09,833
Yeah.

908
00:30:09,867 --> 00:30:12,267
What-- what is this?

909
00:30:12,300 --> 00:30:13,067
-Bryant Curtis?

910
00:30:13,100 --> 00:30:14,300
-"King Dinosaur."

911
00:30:14,333 --> 00:30:14,867
-Oh.

912
00:30:14,900 --> 00:30:15,500
-It's a Lippert film.

913
00:30:15,533 --> 00:30:16,467
[Screaming]

914
00:30:16,500 --> 00:30:17,433
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

915
00:30:17,467 --> 00:30:18,400
We know.

916
00:30:22,200 --> 00:30:23,167
Pretty sad, huh?

917
00:30:23,200 --> 00:30:23,600
-Oh my gosh.

918
00:30:23,633 --> 00:30:24,767
-Marvin Miller?

919
00:30:24,800 --> 00:30:26,733
This must be his crossing.

920
00:30:26,767 --> 00:30:28,433
With the movie and--

921
00:30:28,467 --> 00:30:29,000
-Yeah.

922
00:30:29,033 --> 00:30:29,833
-Joel and Ethan Coen.

923
00:30:33,333 --> 00:30:37,167
There's a-- it's a
Zimbalists in this film.

924
00:30:37,200 --> 00:30:39,467
I think there was some nepotism.

925
00:30:39,500 --> 00:30:41,533
There's Ralph Helfer.

926
00:30:41,567 --> 00:30:43,367
I hear he's a big cow.

927
00:30:43,400 --> 00:30:47,767
Again, with the
heifer and-- sorry.

928
00:30:47,800 --> 00:30:51,867
-Ah, based on an original
story by August Strindberg.

929
00:30:51,900 --> 00:30:54,533
"Beast From Outer
Space," his first wife.

930
00:30:54,567 --> 00:30:56,933
-Tom Gries, and the whole
world Gries with him.

931
00:30:56,967 --> 00:30:59,733
-That's as bad as mine.

932
00:30:59,767 --> 00:31:02,633
-It's Al Bert I.,
and Zimbalist Gordon.

933
00:31:02,667 --> 00:31:03,533
-They're credits.

934
00:31:03,567 --> 00:31:04,600
-Oh, I'm filled with wonder.

935
00:31:04,633 --> 00:31:07,600
These titles have whisked
me away to a magic land.

936
00:31:07,633 --> 00:31:08,867
-Oh, don't go away, titles.

937
00:31:08,900 --> 00:31:10,933
NARRATOR: On April 23, the word
to start comes from Washington.

938
00:31:10,967 --> 00:31:12,100
-Start.

939
00:31:12,133 --> 00:31:13,067
NARRATOR: Immediately, our
finest research laboratories

940
00:31:13,100 --> 00:31:15,633
change over to this
all-important project,

941
00:31:15,667 --> 00:31:18,567
the building of a passenger
carrying rocket ship.

942
00:31:18,600 --> 00:31:19,833
Every security measure is taken.

943
00:31:19,867 --> 00:31:21,733
-What you see, what you hear,
what you leave-- Burma-Shave,

944
00:31:21,767 --> 00:31:22,667
huh.

945
00:31:22,700 --> 00:31:24,333
NARRATOR: Test upon
test must be made

946
00:31:24,367 --> 00:31:25,800
to develop, improve, create--

947
00:31:25,833 --> 00:31:30,467
-The ultimate morning
zoo radio show.

948
00:31:30,500 --> 00:31:32,233
NARRATOR: A jet
engine must be built

949
00:31:32,267 --> 00:31:33,467
with a thrust great
enough to launch

950
00:31:33,500 --> 00:31:35,900
the spaceship through the many
miles of Earth's atmosphere.

951
00:31:35,933 --> 00:31:37,900
-If you lean to the side, this
looks like a burning building.

952
00:31:37,933 --> 00:31:39,867
NARRATOR: --withstand the
pressures found in outer space.

953
00:31:44,033 --> 00:31:46,533
-Then the world's largest
roll-on deodorant was invented.

954
00:31:46,567 --> 00:31:48,367
NARRATOR: --unusally favorable
atmospheric conditions,

955
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:50,867
Professor Albert Garnett is
able with the world's largest

956
00:31:50,900 --> 00:31:53,033
telescope to
photograph an extremely

957
00:31:53,067 --> 00:31:53,867
clear view of a new planet.

958
00:31:53,900 --> 00:31:55,067
-But he had his
thumb over the lens.

959
00:31:55,100 --> 00:31:57,300
NARRATOR: It is now certain
that plant life of some kind

960
00:31:57,333 --> 00:31:58,933
definitely exists there.

961
00:31:58,967 --> 00:32:00,133
-Tony Danza, for instance.

962
00:32:00,133 --> 00:32:01,133
NARRATOR: The
professor, incidentally,

963
00:32:01,133 --> 00:32:02,433
is also credited with
naming the new planet.

964
00:32:02,467 --> 00:32:03,433
-Planet Alan.

965
00:32:03,467 --> 00:32:04,033
NARRATOR: Planet Nova.

966
00:32:04,067 --> 00:32:04,700
-Oh.

967
00:32:04,733 --> 00:32:06,567
-Close.

968
00:32:06,600 --> 00:32:07,933
NARRATOR: New metals
must be developed,

969
00:32:07,967 --> 00:32:10,067
with tensile strength
capable of withstanding

970
00:32:10,100 --> 00:32:13,633
great atmospheric pressures.

971
00:32:13,667 --> 00:32:14,700
-Lumps.

972
00:32:14,733 --> 00:32:16,667
NARRATOR: Switch on for
jet engine test number 87.

973
00:32:16,700 --> 00:32:21,067
-Oh, so the announcer is
calling the shots now?

974
00:32:21,100 --> 00:32:23,533
-Ooh.

975
00:32:23,567 --> 00:32:27,633
-I think their
dryer is off-kilter.

976
00:32:27,667 --> 00:32:31,867
NARRATOR: Structural weaknesses
are studied, tested--

977
00:32:31,900 --> 00:32:34,833
-Oh, here the effects of 13
rum and Diet Cokes are tested.

978
00:32:34,867 --> 00:32:37,467
NARRATOR: Every conceivable
test for man and machine alike

979
00:32:37,500 --> 00:32:38,433
has run the gauntlet.

980
00:32:38,467 --> 00:32:39,867
There is no margin for error.

981
00:32:39,900 --> 00:32:41,667
-There is a margin
for shame, however.

982
00:32:41,700 --> 00:32:42,967
NARRATOR: Special
equipment is developed.

983
00:32:43,000 --> 00:32:43,800
-Then discarded.

984
00:32:43,833 --> 00:32:44,867
NARRATOR: This
nuclear power plant

985
00:32:44,900 --> 00:32:46,867
will serve as an auxiliary
source of electricity

986
00:32:46,900 --> 00:32:48,967
while our people
are on the planet.

987
00:32:49,000 --> 00:32:51,533
Actually, because it is
activated by atomic power--

988
00:32:51,567 --> 00:32:52,733
-Oh, he was just
kidding earlier.

989
00:32:52,767 --> 00:32:54,833
NARRATOR: --for many years
if something were to go wrong

990
00:32:54,867 --> 00:32:56,067
and their return to
earth were delayed.

991
00:32:56,100 --> 00:32:57,733
-Come on, it looks
like a dashboard.

992
00:32:57,767 --> 00:32:58,567
Oh, port mole.

993
00:32:58,600 --> 00:32:59,900
NARRATOR: Naturally,
the people must

994
00:32:59,933 --> 00:33:01,133
be careful in using
such equipment,

995
00:33:01,167 --> 00:33:04,500
because if the atomic power
were allowed to go unharnessed,

996
00:33:04,533 --> 00:33:06,933
an atomic fission
reaction would take place.

997
00:33:06,967 --> 00:33:07,667
-Ooh.

998
00:33:07,733 --> 00:33:08,767
-Ooh.

999
00:33:08,800 --> 00:33:11,067
-Better one?

1000
00:33:11,100 --> 00:33:12,433
Better four?

1001
00:33:12,467 --> 00:33:14,100
-I better reset the watch here.

1002
00:33:14,133 --> 00:33:15,767
-Ah, way off.

1003
00:33:15,800 --> 00:33:17,500
NARRATOR: They, of course,
will understand its use

1004
00:33:17,533 --> 00:33:19,467
and therefore
minimize any danger.

1005
00:33:19,500 --> 00:33:23,033
-The Brady house,
built tall and strong.

1006
00:33:23,067 --> 00:33:24,933
NARRATOR: Pressure
vaults are constructed

1007
00:33:24,967 --> 00:33:27,800
to simulate the
pressures found in space.

1008
00:33:27,833 --> 00:33:30,533
-And the pressures of my
desolate lonely life, sitting

1009
00:33:30,567 --> 00:33:33,567
for hours alone
in a sound booth.

1010
00:33:33,600 --> 00:33:35,833
NARRATOR: Record
every vibrating pulse.

1011
00:33:35,867 --> 00:33:37,067
-Oh, yes!

1012
00:33:37,100 --> 00:33:38,000
-Oh.

1013
00:33:38,033 --> 00:33:39,033
Yes.

1014
00:33:39,067 --> 00:33:40,633
NARRATOR: Turbo jet
engines used in assisting

1015
00:33:40,667 --> 00:33:42,900
takeoffs or our giant
bombers are studied.

1016
00:33:42,933 --> 00:33:44,933
But there lies the
greatest problem.

1017
00:33:44,967 --> 00:33:46,033
Blasting the ship--

1018
00:33:46,067 --> 00:33:47,833
-Uh, looks like he's burning
some oil, doesn't it?

1019
00:33:47,867 --> 00:33:50,633
-Yeah.

1020
00:33:50,667 --> 00:33:52,867
-Then German air
shows are performed.

1021
00:33:52,900 --> 00:33:53,833
Hundreds are killed.

1022
00:33:59,700 --> 00:34:01,600
Hi!

1023
00:34:01,633 --> 00:34:03,067
NARRATOR: The first
test rocket is ready.

1024
00:34:03,100 --> 00:34:05,567
-Switch on for launching.

1025
00:34:05,600 --> 00:34:07,233
-Hey, they flew it out
of the Eiffel Tower.

1026
00:34:07,267 --> 00:34:08,667
NARRATOR: Rocket
test number two.

1027
00:34:08,700 --> 00:34:12,067
-That was number two!

1028
00:34:12,100 --> 00:34:14,833
NARRATOR: This rocket
carries animals, white mice--

1029
00:34:14,867 --> 00:34:17,000
-Hi, my name is Benji, and
I'm a 10-dimensional being.

1030
00:34:17,033 --> 00:34:18,800
NARRATOR: --gravity is still
present to hold the mice

1031
00:34:18,833 --> 00:34:20,300
and little ball
downward toward Earth.

1032
00:34:23,267 --> 00:34:25,133
-Whoa!

1033
00:34:25,167 --> 00:34:26,667
NARRATOR: Now the mice
are floating in air,

1034
00:34:26,700 --> 00:34:29,000
since they are now beyond
Earth's gravity pull.

1035
00:34:29,033 --> 00:34:30,633
-Hey, why do we have
to do all the work?

1036
00:34:30,667 --> 00:34:32,033
I'm not feeling too good.

1037
00:34:32,067 --> 00:34:34,233
NARRATOR: The test proves out.

1038
00:34:34,267 --> 00:34:36,033
Animals can live in space.

1039
00:34:36,067 --> 00:34:36,700
It's man--

1040
00:34:36,733 --> 00:34:37,900
-What are you, man or mouse?

1041
00:34:37,933 --> 00:34:39,767
-Well, put some cheese down
there, and you'll find out.

1042
00:34:39,800 --> 00:34:40,800
NARRATOR: The
spaceship is ready.

1043
00:34:40,833 --> 00:34:44,200
Now for its passengers.

1044
00:34:44,233 --> 00:34:45,433
-Hey, Keith Richards.

1045
00:34:45,467 --> 00:34:47,667
NARRATOR: If animal life is
found on the new planet Nova,

1046
00:34:47,733 --> 00:34:51,000
an expert on zoogeography would
be a most important member

1047
00:34:51,033 --> 00:34:52,100
of the space expedition.

1048
00:34:52,133 --> 00:34:53,067
-Calcified and--

1049
00:34:53,100 --> 00:34:55,033
NARRATOR: On August
10, Dr. Richard Gordon

1050
00:34:55,067 --> 00:34:56,967
was chosen to fill
that position.

1051
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:58,500
He became famous
with his discovery

1052
00:34:58,533 --> 00:35:01,300
of the giant prehistoric tar
pits near Salt Lake City,

1053
00:35:01,333 --> 00:35:02,000
just two years ago.

1054
00:35:02,033 --> 00:35:03,833
-I'd say this patient is dead.

1055
00:35:03,867 --> 00:35:05,933
I'm no expert, but
dead's the word.

1056
00:35:05,967 --> 00:35:07,800
NARRATOR: The study of rock
formations and its minerals

1057
00:35:07,833 --> 00:35:10,167
is like reading the
personal diary of a planet.

1058
00:35:10,200 --> 00:35:11,333
-Yes, it's not very polite.

1059
00:35:11,367 --> 00:35:13,133
NARRATOR: Dr. Nora Pearce
joined the space expedition

1060
00:35:13,167 --> 00:35:14,933
on August 27.

1061
00:35:14,967 --> 00:35:16,700
Her doctorate in
mineralogy was awarded

1062
00:35:16,733 --> 00:35:19,100
for her mineralogical research
in the Himalayan Mountains.

1063
00:35:19,133 --> 00:35:20,933
-And she's cute too.

1064
00:35:20,967 --> 00:35:22,467
NARRATOR: That's the day
the giant spaceship was

1065
00:35:22,500 --> 00:35:23,300
being wheeled into the--

1066
00:35:23,333 --> 00:35:24,100
-Fire.

1067
00:35:24,133 --> 00:35:24,900
NARRATOR: --for
its space flight,

1068
00:35:24,933 --> 00:35:26,300
which was now a
matter of weeks--

1069
00:35:26,333 --> 00:35:27,267
-Painted up by hand.

1070
00:35:27,300 --> 00:35:28,400
-Nice color.

1071
00:35:28,433 --> 00:35:29,033
-Yeah.

1072
00:35:29,067 --> 00:35:29,433
Lovely.

1073
00:35:29,467 --> 00:35:30,633
Great fins.

1074
00:35:30,667 --> 00:35:32,933
NARRATOR: Medicine must be
represented on this expedition.

1075
00:35:32,967 --> 00:35:33,733
-Because they'll need drugs.

1076
00:35:33,767 --> 00:35:34,700
NARRATOR: --people
and the people

1077
00:35:34,733 --> 00:35:36,733
that will venture into
space in the future

1078
00:35:36,767 --> 00:35:37,900
is of primary importance.

1079
00:35:37,933 --> 00:35:39,067
-OK, you may spit now.

1080
00:35:39,100 --> 00:35:41,200
NARRATOR: Dr. Ralph Martin's
war service fortified him

1081
00:35:41,233 --> 00:35:43,300
with the experience of
treating most diseases

1082
00:35:43,333 --> 00:35:44,900
and fatalities
that overtake men.

1083
00:35:44,933 --> 00:35:45,900
-Hey, he's cute too, isn't he?

1084
00:35:45,933 --> 00:35:46,300
-Mm-hmm.

1085
00:35:46,333 --> 00:35:47,133
Mm-hmm.

1086
00:35:47,167 --> 00:35:48,067
-Real cute.

1087
00:35:48,100 --> 00:35:49,067
NARRATOR: The chemistry
of the new planet

1088
00:35:49,100 --> 00:35:51,867
was to be studied by Dr.
Patricia Bennett, who

1089
00:35:51,900 --> 00:35:52,967
completed the group
of scientists.

1090
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:53,867
-Drink it.
Drink it.

1091
00:35:53,900 --> 00:35:54,833
NARRATOR: She was
noted for her thesis

1092
00:35:54,867 --> 00:35:57,133
on the use or
radiochemistry in medicine.

1093
00:35:57,167 --> 00:35:58,600
-And, uh, she's cute too.

1094
00:35:58,633 --> 00:36:01,833
NARRATOR: --on September 12.

1095
00:36:01,867 --> 00:36:03,467
-Hm, dirty.

1096
00:36:03,500 --> 00:36:04,733
NARRATOR: On October
1, it was observed

1097
00:36:04,767 --> 00:36:08,333
that the position of Nova was in
the most advantageous location

1098
00:36:08,367 --> 00:36:09,267
for our purpose.

1099
00:36:09,300 --> 00:36:12,367
The ship must blast
off within 24 hours.

1100
00:36:12,400 --> 00:36:14,100
-Or someone's going to pay.

1101
00:36:14,133 --> 00:36:15,067
Oh, brother.

1102
00:36:18,033 --> 00:36:18,967
-Uh-oh.

1103
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:22,267
Is Kooky the Clown on
the premises again?

1104
00:36:22,300 --> 00:36:23,500
NARRATOR: Attention--

1105
00:36:23,533 --> 00:36:24,533
-It's me, the announcer.

1106
00:36:24,567 --> 00:36:25,733
This time I'm coming
from a different speaker.

1107
00:36:25,767 --> 00:36:29,433
NARRATOR: --the expedition
will please report to the ship.

1108
00:36:29,467 --> 00:36:32,233
Blast off is in X
minus 20 minutes.

1109
00:36:32,267 --> 00:36:34,433
-Will the artist please
come to the stage, please?

1110
00:36:38,067 --> 00:36:40,267
-I wonder what
this button's for.

1111
00:36:43,600 --> 00:36:46,167
NARRATOR: Attention, blast
off in X minus 5 minutes.

1112
00:36:46,200 --> 00:36:47,433
-Hey, look.

1113
00:36:47,467 --> 00:36:50,267
It's Woodstock-- three days of
peace, love, and stock footage.

1114
00:36:50,300 --> 00:36:53,000
-Stay away from the towers.

1115
00:36:53,033 --> 00:36:53,567
-You look neat.

1116
00:36:58,000 --> 00:36:59,067
-10 seconds tick away.

1117
00:36:59,100 --> 00:37:02,267
The stock footage is rolled.

1118
00:37:02,300 --> 00:37:03,533
NARRATOR: Blast off in X minus--

1119
00:37:03,567 --> 00:37:04,533
-All right, all right.

1120
00:37:04,567 --> 00:37:05,100
I know.

1121
00:37:09,300 --> 00:37:10,533
-A new pope is selected.

1122
00:37:10,567 --> 00:37:11,533
It's Kooky.

1123
00:37:11,567 --> 00:37:13,067
-Neat.

1124
00:37:13,100 --> 00:37:14,700
NARRATOR: Five,
four, three, two--

1125
00:37:14,733 --> 00:37:15,500
-Ho, ho, whoa!

1126
00:37:15,533 --> 00:37:16,500
-That's a fast count.

1127
00:37:19,700 --> 00:37:20,833
-Huh.

1128
00:37:20,867 --> 00:37:22,967
Well, maybe you should take
your foot off the clutch.

1129
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:25,333
There you go.

1130
00:37:25,367 --> 00:37:26,700
-It's hard to get
it out of first.

1131
00:37:26,733 --> 00:37:27,633
-Yeah.

1132
00:37:27,667 --> 00:37:28,333
-Whoa.

1133
00:37:28,367 --> 00:37:29,633
-It's a German rocket, you know.

1134
00:37:29,667 --> 00:37:30,600
-Whoa!

1135
00:37:30,633 --> 00:37:32,167
-That's farfegnugen.

1136
00:37:32,200 --> 00:37:33,167
-Whoa!

1137
00:37:33,233 --> 00:37:34,567
Oh, no!

1138
00:37:43,533 --> 00:37:45,500
-I believe by the
end of this decade,

1139
00:37:45,533 --> 00:37:48,400
we will land a piece of stock
footage on another planet.

1140
00:37:48,433 --> 00:37:52,533
Ask not what a process
shot can do for you.

1141
00:37:52,567 --> 00:37:55,067
-Very good.

1142
00:37:55,100 --> 00:37:55,633
-Uh-oh.

1143
00:37:55,667 --> 00:37:57,333
It's a handicap spot.

1144
00:37:57,367 --> 00:37:59,433
Hey, and it's not close
enough to the mall.

1145
00:38:04,200 --> 00:38:06,167
-Guys, I think they
landed in Wisconsin.

1146
00:38:06,200 --> 00:38:07,033
-Yeah.

1147
00:38:07,067 --> 00:38:09,200
Uh, they just discovered
Grizzly Adams.

1148
00:38:12,700 --> 00:38:16,667
-Pff, uh, that's one
small step for a man,

1149
00:38:16,700 --> 00:38:20,500
uh, one giant step
into a cowpie.

1150
00:38:20,533 --> 00:38:21,100
-Ew.

1151
00:38:21,133 --> 00:38:21,500
-Over.

1152
00:38:21,533 --> 00:38:23,233
Pff.

1153
00:38:23,267 --> 00:38:24,433
-Cute, huh?

1154
00:38:24,467 --> 00:38:28,333
-They're going all this way
just to make out in a field?

1155
00:38:28,367 --> 00:38:31,233
-Uh, well--

1156
00:38:31,267 --> 00:38:35,033
-At least their face
shields are interlocking.

1157
00:38:35,067 --> 00:38:36,767
-Hey, Joel, is she a
bubble-headed blonde?

1158
00:38:36,800 --> 00:38:39,700
-Uh, yeah.

1159
00:38:39,733 --> 00:38:40,733
-We're first.

1160
00:38:40,767 --> 00:38:41,800
-And foremost.

1161
00:38:41,833 --> 00:38:43,600
-Thank god we made it.

1162
00:38:43,633 --> 00:38:44,300
-Mm.

1163
00:38:44,333 --> 00:38:45,433
-Is it as lovely as it seems?

1164
00:38:45,467 --> 00:38:45,833
-Well--

1165
00:38:45,867 --> 00:38:46,467
-No.

1166
00:38:46,500 --> 00:38:47,100
-Uh, yeah.

1167
00:38:49,767 --> 00:38:50,667
-No.

1168
00:38:50,700 --> 00:38:52,400
-An active volcano.

1169
00:38:52,433 --> 00:38:54,233
This planet is quite young, Pat.

1170
00:38:54,267 --> 00:38:55,567
-I mean, uh, Dr. Pat.

1171
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:57,300
-They're getting
kind of informal.

1172
00:38:57,333 --> 00:38:57,700
-Yeah.

1173
00:38:57,733 --> 00:38:59,267
-Mm-hmm.

1174
00:38:59,300 --> 00:39:00,133
-Cash.

1175
00:39:00,167 --> 00:39:01,067
-We're going to
start out test, Dick,

1176
00:39:01,100 --> 00:39:01,633
to see if it's safe
to remove our suits.

1177
00:39:01,667 --> 00:39:02,200
-What did he call him?

1178
00:39:02,233 --> 00:39:03,133
-What did he just say?

1179
00:39:03,167 --> 00:39:06,333
-Your instruments are
on their way down.

1180
00:39:06,367 --> 00:39:07,600
Hurry up with the
test, so Nora and I

1181
00:39:07,633 --> 00:39:08,533
can get out and join you, huh?

1182
00:39:08,567 --> 00:39:10,700
-Yeah, we want to
frolic in the raw too.

1183
00:39:13,633 --> 00:39:16,733
-Oh, thank goodness the
time constant is safe.

1184
00:39:16,767 --> 00:39:18,667
-Whoops.

1185
00:39:18,700 --> 00:39:20,633
-Why is he blow drying
the grass, guys?

1186
00:39:20,667 --> 00:39:21,800
-Because he can.

1187
00:39:21,833 --> 00:39:22,433
-Oh.

1188
00:39:26,733 --> 00:39:27,600
-Hey look, honey.

1189
00:39:27,633 --> 00:39:28,867
There's change everywhere.

1190
00:39:28,900 --> 00:39:31,200
This is better
than Coney Island.

1191
00:39:31,233 --> 00:39:35,333
-(ECHOING THE SOUNDTRACK)
Do-do-do-do, Do-do-do.

1192
00:39:35,367 --> 00:39:37,967
-Why, uh, this is smoke-able.

1193
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:38,467
Hm.

1194
00:39:38,500 --> 00:39:41,433
High grade hydro.

1195
00:39:41,467 --> 00:39:42,833
-Chunky black.

1196
00:39:42,867 --> 00:39:45,333
Hm.

1197
00:39:45,367 --> 00:39:47,867
-Hey, I can see my brain.

1198
00:39:47,933 --> 00:39:49,267
-(ECHOING THE
SOUNDTRACK) Do-do-do.

1199
00:39:52,633 --> 00:39:54,800
-Oh, let's see what this one
is-- "Origin of a Planet?"

1200
00:39:54,833 --> 00:39:58,400
Oh, I've got that on CD.

1201
00:39:58,433 --> 00:40:00,967
-(ECHOING THE
SOUNDTRACK) Do-do-do.

1202
00:40:01,000 --> 00:40:02,900
-Is Pete Fountain around here?

1203
00:40:02,933 --> 00:40:06,033
-A preliminary biological
study made of the air on Nova--

1204
00:40:06,067 --> 00:40:07,000
-Who's talking?

1205
00:40:07,033 --> 00:40:08,900
--show the indications
of microscopic life

1206
00:40:08,933 --> 00:40:11,500
not too different from
that found on Earth.

1207
00:40:11,533 --> 00:40:13,767
However, approximately 40% of--

1208
00:40:13,800 --> 00:40:14,633
-Did I bring this with me?

1209
00:40:14,667 --> 00:40:15,500
--the bacteria--

1210
00:40:15,533 --> 00:40:16,200
-Was superimposed.

1211
00:40:16,233 --> 00:40:18,000
--was completely unfamiliar.

1212
00:40:18,033 --> 00:40:21,433
-Test for radiation gave a
reading of approximately 10%

1213
00:40:21,433 --> 00:40:22,967
above normal background count.

1214
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:23,667
-Joel, I'm tripping.

1215
00:40:23,700 --> 00:40:25,867
-No, it's just a photo process.

1216
00:40:25,900 --> 00:40:26,833
-A Coppola film.

1217
00:40:26,867 --> 00:40:28,833
-Yeah.

1218
00:40:28,867 --> 00:40:30,067
-Temperature--

1219
00:40:30,100 --> 00:40:30,967
-Expletive deleted.

1220
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:33,467
18 minutes was deleted
from this tape.

1221
00:40:33,500 --> 00:40:35,667
I am not a crook.

1222
00:40:35,700 --> 00:40:38,933
-Although our preliminary tests
are in no way conclusive--

1223
00:40:38,967 --> 00:40:40,133
-Or any good at all.

1224
00:40:40,167 --> 00:40:43,767
--it does substantiate, however,
that human and animal life

1225
00:40:43,800 --> 00:40:46,867
as we know it on Earth can
exist in the atmosphere

1226
00:40:46,900 --> 00:40:49,500
and environment of Planet Nova.

1227
00:40:49,533 --> 00:40:50,933
-The end.

1228
00:40:50,967 --> 00:40:53,733
-Oh, they taped over the
Beatles Berlin tapes.

1229
00:40:53,733 --> 00:40:57,533
[Groaning]

1230
00:40:57,567 --> 00:40:58,967
-Now, lets get out
of these suits.

1231
00:40:59,000 --> 00:41:00,767
-And into a dry martini.

1232
00:41:00,800 --> 00:41:03,000
-Nora, the atmosphere
checks out all right.

1233
00:41:03,033 --> 00:41:04,933
It's safe to come out.

1234
00:41:04,967 --> 00:41:05,567
-Hm.

1235
00:41:05,600 --> 00:41:08,167
He means, uh-- high five.

1236
00:41:08,200 --> 00:41:09,400
Oh, that's Patty-cake.

1237
00:41:09,433 --> 00:41:13,467
That's the international symbol
for "off with your clothes."

1238
00:41:13,500 --> 00:41:15,067
-Oh, what a day.

1239
00:41:15,100 --> 00:41:17,833
Patty, I'll cover your rear.

1240
00:41:26,200 --> 00:41:27,933
-The air's a bit
richer in oxygen.

1241
00:41:27,967 --> 00:41:28,567
Notice?

1242
00:41:28,633 --> 00:41:30,533
-Yep, I'm high as a kite.

1243
00:41:30,567 --> 00:41:32,800
-Anyway, it'll speed
up our reaction.

1244
00:41:32,833 --> 00:41:34,467
-Oh, you take care of
your own reaction, doctor.

1245
00:41:34,500 --> 00:41:35,500
-Ho-ho.

1246
00:41:35,533 --> 00:41:36,200
-What do you think Nora?

1247
00:41:36,233 --> 00:41:37,200
-This is just great.

1248
00:41:37,233 --> 00:41:37,867
[Laughter]

1249
00:41:37,900 --> 00:41:39,933
-There's animal life here.

1250
00:41:39,967 --> 00:41:40,367
-Yeah.

1251
00:41:40,400 --> 00:41:42,067
White caribou.

1252
00:41:42,100 --> 00:41:43,067
-Cute.

1253
00:41:43,100 --> 00:41:44,133
-Listen, I saw a lake
over in that direction

1254
00:41:44,167 --> 00:41:45,833
before we landed.
Let's go over and take a look.

1255
00:41:45,867 --> 00:41:46,833
-Ok.

1256
00:41:46,867 --> 00:41:49,000
-I think you mean you
saw a clip of a lake.

1257
00:41:49,033 --> 00:41:50,233
-Everyone got a date?

1258
00:41:50,267 --> 00:41:52,533
Let's have that progressive
dinner we were talking about,

1259
00:41:52,567 --> 00:41:54,600
huh?

1260
00:41:54,633 --> 00:41:57,200
Let's just throw all the
research out the window.

1261
00:41:57,233 --> 00:41:58,233
-Look, birds.

1262
00:41:58,267 --> 00:41:59,600
-They look like vultures.

1263
00:41:59,633 --> 00:42:01,033
-Ah, death loving vultures.

1264
00:42:01,033 --> 00:42:02,067
Hm, neat.

1265
00:42:06,600 --> 00:42:08,233
-Look at that three.

1266
00:42:08,267 --> 00:42:10,167
-Ha, wish I could do that.

1267
00:42:10,200 --> 00:42:12,200
-Isn't he cute?

1268
00:42:12,233 --> 00:42:13,067
-Bam, bam!

1269
00:42:18,633 --> 00:42:21,600
-Well, there it is, just
like I saw on the scope.

1270
00:42:21,633 --> 00:42:24,833
As pretty a lake as anyone would
want, and it's all private.

1271
00:42:24,867 --> 00:42:25,400
-Restricted.

1272
00:42:25,433 --> 00:42:26,800
-What about that island?

1273
00:42:26,833 --> 00:42:27,700
-Oh.

1274
00:42:27,733 --> 00:42:29,100
-That's a strange-looking place.

1275
00:42:29,133 --> 00:42:31,233
-Wow, it must be scary.

1276
00:42:31,267 --> 00:42:32,633
-No sun over there.

1277
00:42:32,667 --> 00:42:33,700
-No son of mine.

1278
00:42:33,733 --> 00:42:35,567
-I'd like to have a look
at that when we have time.

1279
00:42:35,600 --> 00:42:37,033
-I don't see why, Nora.

1280
00:42:37,067 --> 00:42:39,733
All they want in Washington
are tests and samples

1281
00:42:39,767 --> 00:42:40,433
from wherever we land.

1282
00:42:40,467 --> 00:42:41,600
-Well, if you need souvenirs.

1283
00:42:41,633 --> 00:42:43,267
-They didn't say anything
about extensive exploration.

1284
00:42:43,300 --> 00:42:45,133
-You conservative doctors.

1285
00:42:45,167 --> 00:42:47,067
-Hey, we're
neoconservative doctors.

1286
00:42:47,100 --> 00:42:48,267
-I don't care.

1287
00:42:48,300 --> 00:42:51,200
All I can see is that water,
and that means a bath, Nora.

1288
00:42:51,233 --> 00:42:54,000
And let's get some clean
clothes and have one, huh?

1289
00:42:54,033 --> 00:42:55,600
[Giggling]

1290
00:42:55,633 --> 00:42:56,300
-Oh, for fun.

1291
00:42:56,333 --> 00:42:56,867
-Ah-ah!

1292
00:42:56,900 --> 00:42:57,667
Ladies first, please.

1293
00:42:57,700 --> 00:42:58,900
[Giggling]

1294
00:42:58,933 --> 00:42:59,800
-Ok.

1295
00:42:59,833 --> 00:43:01,100
You've got to love them.

1296
00:43:01,133 --> 00:43:02,100
Let's go.

1297
00:43:06,267 --> 00:43:07,967
-Who brought the sandwiches?

1298
00:43:08,033 --> 00:43:08,633
-Sandwiches?

1299
00:43:11,933 --> 00:43:13,167
-Ooh.

1300
00:43:13,200 --> 00:43:15,633
Quite a package he's got there.

1301
00:43:15,667 --> 00:43:16,067
-Mm-hmm.

1302
00:43:21,233 --> 00:43:22,367
-Much further?

1303
00:43:22,400 --> 00:43:26,733
-I want some samples from
that rock straight up there.

1304
00:43:26,767 --> 00:43:28,733
-I'm Chirpy, the
mutant hell beast,

1305
00:43:28,767 --> 00:43:30,233
