to the big snowmobile show.

2326
01:20:46,833 --> 01:20:47,600
-Thank you.

2327
01:20:47,633 --> 01:20:49,333
You know how high
that flare would

2328
01:20:49,367 --> 01:20:50,867
have to have gone
for them to see it?

2329
01:20:50,900 --> 01:20:51,867
Can you believe this?

2330
01:20:57,267 --> 01:20:59,600
-Throw it into the-- [HISSING].

2331
01:20:59,633 --> 01:21:01,700
-You know, Tom, if you're
looking for plausibility

2332
01:21:01,733 --> 01:21:04,000
in this film, you won't
find it here, friend.

2333
01:21:04,033 --> 01:21:05,333
-You're right.

2334
01:21:05,367 --> 01:21:08,467
-Hey, they made it already.

2335
01:21:08,500 --> 01:21:09,733
[Roaring]

2336
01:21:09,767 --> 01:21:12,733
-It resembles the Tyrannosaurus
Rex of Earth's prehistoric age.

2337
01:21:12,767 --> 01:21:13,833
-No, it doesn't.

2338
01:21:13,867 --> 01:21:15,367
-Sorry, no way.

2339
01:21:15,400 --> 01:21:16,700
King Dinosaur.

2340
01:21:16,733 --> 01:21:18,367
-Have breakfast with the king.

2341
01:21:18,400 --> 01:21:21,833
-Sorry, that's a
lizard from Pet World.

2342
01:21:21,867 --> 01:21:25,700
--just as similar ones dominated
Earth 125 million years ago.

2343
01:21:25,733 --> 01:21:26,967
It's like living in the past.

2344
01:21:27,000 --> 01:21:28,633
-It's an iguana.

2345
01:21:28,667 --> 01:21:29,267
-Come on.

2346
01:21:29,300 --> 01:21:31,933
-Good likeness, isn't it?

2347
01:21:31,967 --> 01:21:33,500
-Well, who cares?

2348
01:21:33,533 --> 01:21:35,900
No one's ever going to believe
us anyway, if we ever--

2349
01:21:35,933 --> 01:21:37,500
[laughter]

2350
01:21:37,533 --> 01:21:39,233
-That's a scientist for you.

2351
01:21:39,267 --> 01:21:39,933
-Hey, help me.

2352
01:21:39,967 --> 01:21:42,767
I don't like it when you fight.

2353
01:21:42,800 --> 01:21:44,367
-Meanwhile, three days later.

2354
01:21:44,400 --> 01:21:47,500
-And anyway, there's some
real big jacks on this lake,

2355
01:21:47,533 --> 01:21:50,300
and I'm sure that
if we just get some

2356
01:21:50,333 --> 01:21:51,867
time to drown a few slugs--

2357
01:21:51,900 --> 01:21:52,533
-Hey, look.

2358
01:21:52,567 --> 01:21:53,467
Lemur scat.

2359
01:21:53,500 --> 01:21:55,800
We must be getting close.

2360
01:21:55,833 --> 01:21:57,033
-Looks like Bob's.

2361
01:21:57,067 --> 01:21:59,900
-Listen to this music.

2362
01:21:59,933 --> 01:22:02,333
-What of Paul's love for Lena?

2363
01:22:02,367 --> 01:22:04,433
Will the lizards find
peace in the valley?

2364
01:22:04,467 --> 01:22:07,700
And what of little
Joey, the lemur?

2365
01:22:07,733 --> 01:22:09,000
You'll find out this and more--

2366
01:22:12,733 --> 01:22:15,000
-What's he gonna do,
gull him to death?

2367
01:22:15,033 --> 01:22:17,733
-Ah, let me in there.

2368
01:22:17,767 --> 01:22:18,900
-If you come out
of the woods today,

2369
01:22:18,933 --> 01:22:20,600
you're in for a big surprise.

2370
01:22:20,633 --> 01:22:22,033
If you're-- come
on, keep up with me.

2371
01:22:22,067 --> 01:22:24,333
You go into the woods today,
you won't believe your eyes.

2372
01:22:24,367 --> 01:22:24,767
[Humming].

2373
01:22:28,467 --> 01:22:30,533
Teddy bears having a picnic.

2374
01:22:30,567 --> 01:22:32,867
-You know, they're
gonna be there tomorrow

2375
01:22:32,900 --> 01:22:34,333
afternoon at this pace.

2376
01:22:34,367 --> 01:22:35,733
-La, la, la.

2377
01:22:35,767 --> 01:22:37,733
-La, la, la.

2378
01:22:37,767 --> 01:22:39,800
-Who's this guy?

2379
01:22:39,833 --> 01:22:40,933
I don't know.

2380
01:22:40,967 --> 01:22:42,667
-And that's the day the teddy
bears have their picnic.

2381
01:22:46,100 --> 01:22:46,767
-Take my hand.

2382
01:22:46,800 --> 01:22:49,033
I'm a stranger with parasites.

2383
01:22:49,067 --> 01:22:52,467
-Why do you have to bring
your shoes everywhere?

2384
01:22:52,500 --> 01:22:53,467
-Give me that photo.

2385
01:22:53,500 --> 01:22:54,667
It's not a good likeness.

2386
01:22:54,700 --> 01:22:56,400
It looks like my passport photo.

2387
01:23:01,867 --> 01:23:02,467
-Mm.

2388
01:23:05,867 --> 01:23:08,767
-That's the day the
teddy bears-- huh?

2389
01:23:08,800 --> 01:23:09,433
-We're closer.

2390
01:23:09,467 --> 01:23:10,633
-Yeah, the thing's lighter.

2391
01:23:10,633 --> 01:23:11,567
-In that canyon.

2392
01:23:11,600 --> 01:23:13,167
-Snack canyon.

2393
01:23:13,200 --> 01:23:14,500
-If you go out into
the woods today,

2394
01:23:14,533 --> 01:23:15,567
you're in for a big surprise.

2395
01:23:15,600 --> 01:23:16,100
-Oh come on, shut up.

2396
01:23:16,133 --> 01:23:17,100
I need that song.

2397
01:23:17,133 --> 01:23:18,767
-Well, you're the one
who started singing it.

2398
01:23:18,800 --> 01:23:19,200
-No, I didn't.

2399
01:23:19,233 --> 01:23:19,767
-Yes, you did.

2400
01:23:19,800 --> 01:23:20,767
-Hey, come on, you guys.

2401
01:23:20,800 --> 01:23:21,700
Stop fighting.

2402
01:23:21,733 --> 01:23:22,267
-Sorry.

2403
01:23:22,300 --> 01:23:24,033
I was doing a callback.

2404
01:23:24,067 --> 01:23:25,867
[Chuckling]

2405
01:23:25,900 --> 01:23:26,500
-Nyah, nyah.

2406
01:23:29,200 --> 01:23:29,600
-Sorry.

2407
01:23:33,633 --> 01:23:34,500
-Huh?
-What?

2408
01:23:34,533 --> 01:23:35,033
What's going-- oh.

2409
01:23:38,167 --> 01:23:39,500
-Oh Ralph, what is it?

2410
01:23:39,533 --> 01:23:40,033
-It's an iguana.

2411
01:23:40,067 --> 01:23:40,767
Now, shut up.

2412
01:23:40,800 --> 01:23:41,867
-I don't know.

2413
01:23:41,900 --> 01:23:42,867
Something prehistoric.

2414
01:23:42,933 --> 01:23:43,767
-Oh, it's horrible.

2415
01:23:43,833 --> 01:23:46,267
-Oh, why do you have
to be so negative.

2416
01:23:46,300 --> 01:23:47,200
-Shoot it with something.

2417
01:23:47,233 --> 01:23:47,767
-What with?

2418
01:23:47,800 --> 01:23:50,700
That won't do any good.

2419
01:23:50,733 --> 01:23:53,167
-Whoa.

2420
01:23:53,200 --> 01:23:54,700
[Yawning]

2421
01:23:58,000 --> 01:23:58,933
-Hey, let go of my tail.

2422
01:23:58,967 --> 01:24:01,267
I want to get down there
and mix it up a bit.

2423
01:24:01,300 --> 01:24:02,833
-Well, do something.

2424
01:24:02,867 --> 01:24:03,467
-Anything.

2425
01:24:03,500 --> 01:24:04,633
-We've got to get to them.

2426
01:24:04,667 --> 01:24:07,233
-No, they've got to get to us.

2427
01:24:07,267 --> 01:24:07,800
-OK, Joel.

2428
01:24:07,833 --> 01:24:09,367
I got $5 on the lizard.

2429
01:24:09,400 --> 01:24:10,300
-Get your money out.

2430
01:24:10,333 --> 01:24:11,533
-OK, I'll take your money.

2431
01:24:11,567 --> 01:24:12,367
OK, no problem.

2432
01:24:12,400 --> 01:24:13,833
Hey Crow, you're
witness to that, OK?

2433
01:24:13,867 --> 01:24:14,833
-Hey, I want in on this action.

2434
01:24:14,867 --> 01:24:16,100
-Ok.

2435
01:24:16,133 --> 01:24:17,700
-I'll bet 10 that they're are
killed at the end of the film.

2436
01:24:17,733 --> 01:24:18,767
-OK.
I'm holding the money.

2437
01:24:18,800 --> 01:24:19,600
-Wait a minute.

2438
01:24:19,633 --> 01:24:20,567
Wait a minute.
What about our bet?

2439
01:24:20,600 --> 01:24:21,567
That's not fair.
-Hey, you don't understand.

2440
01:24:21,600 --> 01:24:23,933
I'm playing the part of
the expensive middleman.

2441
01:24:23,967 --> 01:24:25,333
This way, we all win.

2442
01:24:25,367 --> 01:24:26,100
-Oh.

2443
01:24:26,133 --> 01:24:26,733
-Huh?

2444
01:24:35,633 --> 01:24:38,633
-Buddy, if you pick at
it, it'll never heal.

2445
01:24:38,667 --> 01:24:40,833
Whoa.

2446
01:24:40,867 --> 01:24:42,100
-Oh.

2447
01:24:42,133 --> 01:24:43,633
-Ooh, Jesus.

2448
01:24:43,667 --> 01:24:44,200
-Ah, come on, lizard.

2449
01:24:44,233 --> 01:24:44,900
Come on.

2450
01:24:44,933 --> 01:24:45,600
Daddy needs a new pair of shoes.

2451
01:24:45,633 --> 01:24:47,333
Come on, I got money on this.

2452
01:24:47,367 --> 01:24:49,633
-Luck, be a lizard tonight.

2453
01:24:49,633 --> 01:24:51,833
Come on.

2454
01:24:51,867 --> 01:24:52,467
-Help.

2455
01:24:52,500 --> 01:24:53,767
I'm hooked in your braces.

2456
01:24:53,800 --> 01:24:56,233
[Muffled]

2457
01:24:56,267 --> 01:25:00,633
-I saw this on
"Midnight Express" once.

2458
01:25:00,667 --> 01:25:01,133
-Ouch.

2459
01:25:03,767 --> 01:25:07,433
-Oh, that'd be a lot
more fun on a toboggan.

2460
01:25:07,467 --> 01:25:09,300
-That sounds like
lizards dropping.

2461
01:25:11,967 --> 01:25:14,000
-Sorry I tore your picture.

2462
01:25:14,033 --> 01:25:15,400
-Sorry I ripped your dress.

2463
01:25:15,433 --> 01:25:17,733
-Sorry I smashed your lunchbox.

2464
01:25:17,767 --> 01:25:19,200
-Sorry I broke your heart.

2465
01:25:22,067 --> 01:25:26,100
-Giant mutated lizards-- wish
we had some pictures of it.

2466
01:25:26,133 --> 01:25:28,233
-Doh!

2467
01:25:28,267 --> 01:25:30,267
-They're running through
dino-scat over there.

2468
01:25:30,300 --> 01:25:31,133
-Faster.

2469
01:25:31,167 --> 01:25:31,833
-Up yours.

2470
01:25:31,867 --> 01:25:33,867
I mean, we'll be right there.

2471
01:25:37,033 --> 01:25:39,033
[Groaning]

2472
01:25:39,067 --> 01:25:41,467
-I don't care if you are a
lizard, that has to hurt.

2473
01:25:41,500 --> 01:25:42,433
-Oh, man.

2474
01:25:42,467 --> 01:25:44,400
That's got to really sting.

2475
01:25:47,300 --> 01:25:50,167
-Get up there, you
stupid culotted little--

2476
01:25:50,200 --> 01:25:50,667
-Oh, Ralph.

2477
01:25:50,700 --> 01:25:51,367
Will they make it?

2478
01:25:51,400 --> 01:25:53,100
-Sure, they're going to make it.

2479
01:25:53,133 --> 01:25:54,200
-Or they're going to die trying.

2480
01:25:54,233 --> 01:25:54,833
-Come on.

2481
01:25:57,533 --> 01:26:01,233
-Come on, you buttheads.

2482
01:26:01,267 --> 01:26:03,400
-You know, guys,
lizards were hurt

2483
01:26:03,433 --> 01:26:05,267
in the making of this film.

2484
01:26:05,300 --> 01:26:07,833
-Ah!

2485
01:26:07,867 --> 01:26:08,967
-That's almost a reversal.

2486
01:26:09,000 --> 01:26:11,033
That would have been two points.

2487
01:26:11,067 --> 01:26:13,167
-Oh, I'm filled with shame.

2488
01:26:13,200 --> 01:26:15,167
Oh, Rob.

2489
01:26:15,200 --> 01:26:20,567
-All right, now that that's
done, where's Lipper.t

2490
01:26:20,600 --> 01:26:22,300
I'm coming for you.

2491
01:26:22,333 --> 01:26:23,933
Payback time.

2492
01:26:23,967 --> 01:26:25,167
-Yeah.

2493
01:26:25,200 --> 01:26:29,033
-Looks like a
Cajun cooking show.

2494
01:26:29,067 --> 01:26:29,667
-Oh, honey.

2495
01:26:29,700 --> 01:26:30,500
-I brought the atom bomb.

2496
01:26:30,533 --> 01:26:31,367
I think it's a good
time to use it.

2497
01:26:31,433 --> 01:26:34,600
-Oh, that's your
answer to everything.

2498
01:26:34,633 --> 01:26:36,400
just use the atom bomb.

2499
01:26:36,433 --> 01:26:39,900
-Hey, what's going
on, peace friends?

2500
01:26:39,933 --> 01:26:40,833
-It's set for 8 o'clock.

2501
01:26:40,867 --> 01:26:43,100
-And right now, it's
30 seconds to 8:00.

2502
01:26:43,133 --> 01:26:44,400
-Whoa!

2503
01:26:44,433 --> 01:26:46,833
-Oh, it's a joke.

2504
01:26:46,867 --> 01:26:48,967
-(LAUGHING) I win.

2505
01:26:49,000 --> 01:26:50,167
Ha, ha, ha!

2506
01:26:50,200 --> 01:26:52,333
And I know where you live.

2507
01:26:52,367 --> 01:26:56,067
-Whoa-ho, wrong turn, friends.

2508
01:26:56,100 --> 01:26:57,867
-Ah, big and-- ah, yes.

2509
01:26:57,900 --> 01:26:58,933
-Hey, what did he ever do?

2510
01:26:58,967 --> 01:27:00,067
-He was on the
cover of "Tarkus."

2511
01:27:00,067 --> 01:27:00,433
-Oh, OK.

2512
01:27:00,467 --> 01:27:03,167
Shoot him.

2513
01:27:03,233 --> 01:27:04,700
[Imitating soundtrack]

2514
01:27:04,733 --> 01:27:07,533
-Na, na, na-- this music
sounds like it was written

2515
01:27:07,567 --> 01:27:09,400
by Lori Strong,
you know the girl

2516
01:27:09,433 --> 01:27:11,300
who teased me in third grade.

2517
01:27:11,333 --> 01:27:14,033
-Even the musicians
are mocking this film.

2518
01:27:14,067 --> 01:27:16,367
-Nyah, nyah, nyah--

2519
01:27:16,400 --> 01:27:19,933
-Oh, it sounded
like an airplane.

2520
01:27:19,967 --> 01:27:21,167
-Oh, I just want to be friends.

2521
01:27:21,200 --> 01:27:22,367
Please come back.

2522
01:27:22,400 --> 01:27:25,000
My friends.

2523
01:27:25,033 --> 01:27:28,133
-You know, sometimes
my own species

2524
01:27:28,167 --> 01:27:31,200
makes me feel ashamed, what
they're doing to this dinosaur.

2525
01:27:31,233 --> 01:27:32,267
-Come on, let's go.

2526
01:27:32,300 --> 01:27:32,900
-Whoa.

2527
01:27:37,233 --> 01:27:38,367
-I'm your new boyfriend now.

2528
01:27:38,400 --> 01:27:40,267
Ha, ha.

2529
01:27:40,300 --> 01:27:41,567
-Everybody panic.

2530
01:27:41,600 --> 01:27:42,833
[Screaming]

2531
01:27:42,867 --> 01:27:46,467
-When in danger or in doubt, run
in circles, scream and shout.

2532
01:27:46,500 --> 01:27:47,967
Whoa!

2533
01:27:48,000 --> 01:27:49,667
Help me.

2534
01:27:49,700 --> 01:27:51,600
-There they go.

2535
01:27:51,633 --> 01:27:53,700
-Calm and reassured.

2536
01:27:53,733 --> 01:27:56,233
-Nyah, nyah, nyah--

2537
01:27:56,267 --> 01:27:57,667
-Oh, welcome to Pamplona.

2538
01:27:57,700 --> 01:27:59,400
-This week on
"Living Dangerously,"

2539
01:27:59,433 --> 01:28:01,133
hand-delivered atomic bombs.

2540
01:28:01,167 --> 01:28:03,267
-Green alligators,
long-necked geese, and--

2541
01:28:03,300 --> 01:28:06,233
-Monkey-back camel, and I
saw chimpanzees up there.

2542
01:28:06,267 --> 01:28:10,833
-I'm coming, beaty boy, ooh-hoo.

2543
01:28:10,867 --> 01:28:11,367
-Wow.

2544
01:28:11,433 --> 01:28:13,267
-Get a load of this.

2545
01:28:13,300 --> 01:28:14,700
How did they get him
to shake like that?

2546
01:28:14,733 --> 01:28:17,433
-They covered him
with shag carpeting.

2547
01:28:17,467 --> 01:28:19,567
-Poor elephant.

2548
01:28:19,600 --> 01:28:22,367
-I'm not an animal.

2549
01:28:22,400 --> 01:28:24,100
-It's a good shot.

2550
01:28:24,133 --> 01:28:25,233
[Shout]

2551
01:28:25,267 --> 01:28:26,333
-If you go out in
the woods today,

2552
01:28:26,367 --> 01:28:27,533
you're in for a big surprise.

2553
01:28:27,567 --> 01:28:28,433
Heh.

2554
01:28:28,467 --> 01:28:29,767
-Save yourselves, man.

2555
01:28:29,800 --> 01:28:31,600
There's a bomb back there.

2556
01:28:34,500 --> 01:28:35,200
-Nyah, nyah, nyah--

2557
01:28:38,167 --> 01:28:41,133
-Ugh.

2558
01:28:41,167 --> 01:28:45,267
-This is an exciting run.

2559
01:28:45,300 --> 01:28:45,833
-Whoa.

2560
01:28:45,867 --> 01:28:47,567
-Hey, it's the MGM lion.

2561
01:28:47,600 --> 01:28:50,867
-No, it's the Lippert lizard.

2562
01:28:50,900 --> 01:28:52,500
-OK, you beat me.

2563
01:28:52,533 --> 01:28:54,267
Get out of here, you bums.

2564
01:28:54,300 --> 01:28:55,200
Take--

2565
01:28:55,233 --> 01:28:58,333
-Why can't they stop
hurting each other?

2566
01:28:58,367 --> 01:28:59,600
Come on, man.

2567
01:29:02,500 --> 01:29:05,233
[Imitating soundtrack]

2568
01:29:05,267 --> 01:29:05,700
-Jeepers, friends.

2569
01:29:05,733 --> 01:29:06,367
Wait for me.

2570
01:29:06,400 --> 01:29:08,500
-I was wondering about him.

2571
01:29:08,533 --> 01:29:10,233
-To the beach!

2572
01:29:10,267 --> 01:29:12,100
Kind of like Dunkirk,
isn't it, guys?

2573
01:29:16,333 --> 01:29:17,367
-I'm just a poor turtle.

2574
01:29:17,433 --> 01:29:18,733
Help, Mr. Wizard.

2575
01:29:18,767 --> 01:29:21,300
Help.

2576
01:29:21,333 --> 01:29:22,300
-Hey!

2577
01:29:22,333 --> 01:29:23,300
-Hey!

2578
01:29:23,333 --> 01:29:25,267
-Hey!

2579
01:29:25,300 --> 01:29:26,600
-They're leaving
the other boats.

2580
01:29:26,633 --> 01:29:28,733
-Uh, I think that's
for the lemur.

2581
01:29:28,767 --> 01:29:31,433
-Oh.

2582
01:29:31,467 --> 01:29:36,933
-Stroke, stroke, stroke, stroke.

2583
01:29:36,967 --> 01:29:37,867
-Hey, get me.

2584
01:29:37,900 --> 01:29:38,933
I'm a Hasidic rabbi.

2585
01:29:38,967 --> 01:29:39,567
Shalom.

2586
01:29:43,233 --> 01:29:46,967
-Kind of like you're
on "Hawaii 5-0!"

2587
01:29:47,000 --> 01:29:48,433
-Ah, phooey.

2588
01:29:48,467 --> 01:29:49,833
No one got the joke.

2589
01:29:52,867 --> 01:29:55,400
-I think he's just scooping
water into the boat there.

2590
01:29:58,300 --> 01:29:58,700
-Hm.

2591
01:30:01,967 --> 01:30:02,867
-Hey, anybody have the time?

2592
01:30:02,900 --> 01:30:05,433
There's a little matter
of an atomic bomb.

2593
01:30:05,467 --> 01:30:06,433
-Oh, yeah.

2594
01:30:06,467 --> 01:30:09,333
-(SINGING) Your careers
are finally almost over.

2595
01:30:09,367 --> 01:30:12,000
You'll never be in another film.

2596
01:30:12,033 --> 01:30:14,933
You may end up in the
"Psychotronic Film Guide."

2597
01:30:14,967 --> 01:30:17,033
-Huh?

2598
01:30:17,067 --> 01:30:19,500
-Hey, isn't that
where Bob upchucked?

2599
01:30:19,533 --> 01:30:21,600
It looks like the
Valdez has been here.

2600
01:30:21,633 --> 01:30:26,500
And there's something-- I can
taste carrots in the water.

2601
01:30:26,533 --> 01:30:28,433
[Imitating soundtrack]

2602
01:30:31,600 --> 01:30:32,867
-Huh?

2603
01:30:32,900 --> 01:30:35,733
Uh-oh.

2604
01:30:35,767 --> 01:30:37,567
You know, I think
the film on this lake

2605
01:30:37,600 --> 01:30:38,900
is better than the
film we're watching.

2606
01:30:42,433 --> 01:30:44,667
-Look, he's pushing her
down again for no reason.

2607
01:30:44,700 --> 01:30:46,033
Look at him.

2608
01:30:46,067 --> 01:30:49,567
That's terrible.

2609
01:30:49,600 --> 01:30:50,833
-Just tear out of there.

2610
01:30:50,867 --> 01:30:52,367
-Let's go.

2611
01:30:52,400 --> 01:30:52,933
-Hurry up.

2612
01:30:52,967 --> 01:30:53,667
-Cheese it, everyone.

2613
01:30:53,700 --> 01:30:55,000
-Don't forget me.

2614
01:30:55,033 --> 01:30:55,900
Please.

2615
01:30:55,933 --> 01:30:59,067
-Oh, they tripped
over a burial mound.

2616
01:30:59,100 --> 01:30:59,933
How fitting.

2617
01:30:59,967 --> 01:31:03,167
-You should just
pull her by her hair.

2618
01:31:03,200 --> 01:31:03,767
-Oops.

2619
01:31:03,800 --> 01:31:06,133
-Damn.

2620
01:31:06,167 --> 01:31:08,600
-Ahh!

2621
01:31:08,633 --> 01:31:10,200
-That's the biggest
firework of all.

2622
01:31:10,233 --> 01:31:13,433
-Looks like the beginning
of "Petticoat Junction."

2623
01:31:13,467 --> 01:31:15,633
Petticoat Armageddon.

2624
01:31:15,667 --> 01:31:18,067
-Shouldn't they be covering
their faces or something?

2625
01:31:18,100 --> 01:31:19,667
Or shields or--

2626
01:31:19,700 --> 01:31:21,967
-Uh, why?

2627
01:31:22,000 --> 01:31:22,667
-I don't know.

2628
01:31:22,700 --> 01:31:23,700
It just--

2629
01:31:23,733 --> 01:31:24,967
-Nuclear blast fallout?

2630
01:31:25,000 --> 01:31:26,600
-Yeah.

2631
01:31:26,633 --> 01:31:29,000
-Uh, we did the right
thing, didn't we?

2632
01:31:29,033 --> 01:31:30,867
They'd never surrender.

2633
01:31:30,933 --> 01:31:32,167
It was right for
us to blow them up.

2634
01:31:32,200 --> 01:31:34,533
-Thanks for annihilating
everything I know.

2635
01:31:37,767 --> 01:31:39,700
-You know, they're only
two miles from the blast.

2636
01:31:39,733 --> 01:31:41,033
Aren't they going
to get vaporized?

2637
01:31:41,067 --> 01:31:42,833
-I hope so.

2638
01:31:42,867 --> 01:31:44,533
Well, it's an atom
bomb light, actually.

2639
01:31:44,567 --> 01:31:46,467
1/3 as destructive
as our other bomb.

2640
01:31:46,500 --> 01:31:46,867
-Oh.

2641
01:31:49,933 --> 01:31:53,000
-Well, score one for Earth.

2642
01:31:53,033 --> 01:31:56,067
-Well, we've done it.

2643
01:31:56,100 --> 01:31:59,533
-Not a word of this gets out to
the people on Earth, all right?

2644
01:31:59,567 --> 01:32:01,167
-We sure have done it.

2645
01:32:01,200 --> 01:32:05,633
We brought civilization
to Planet Nova.

2646
01:32:05,667 --> 01:32:06,933
-Ha, was that irony?

2647
01:32:06,967 --> 01:32:07,500
-Come on.

2648
01:32:07,533 --> 01:32:08,233
-This late in the film?

2649
01:32:08,267 --> 01:32:10,733
-Let's go home.

2650
01:32:10,767 --> 01:32:13,967
-Yeah, let's go home and
grow some three-headed kids.

2651
01:32:14,000 --> 01:32:16,733
-Hey, there's a certain
glow about June.

2652
01:32:16,767 --> 01:32:20,900
Heh-- it's a-- never mind.

2653
01:32:20,933 --> 01:32:23,000
-And so with peace
in their hearts

2654
01:32:23,033 --> 01:32:25,733
and fiery death in the
sky, they went home.

2655
01:32:30,233 --> 01:32:32,033
-Kinda pretty in an ugly way.

2656
01:32:32,067 --> 01:32:33,733
-I like it.

2657
01:32:33,767 --> 01:32:34,167
Bah.

2658
01:32:59,767 --> 01:33:00,733
-Hey--

2659
01:33:00,767 --> 01:33:03,200
-You guys, that's the
fourth Robert Lippert

2660
01:33:03,233 --> 01:33:05,333
film we've seen
in as many months.

2661
01:33:05,367 --> 01:33:08,800
Remember "Rocketship
XM," "Jungle Goddess,"

2662
01:33:08,833 --> 01:33:12,300
"Lost Continent," and now
this one, "King Dinosaur."

2663
01:33:12,333 --> 01:33:15,233
I've never even seen
a Kurosawa film.

2664
01:33:15,267 --> 01:33:16,633
Works of Francois Truffaut?

2665
01:33:16,667 --> 01:33:17,200
Uh-uh.

2666
01:33:17,233 --> 01:33:17,900
Not familiar with them.

2667
01:33:17,933 --> 01:33:18,467
Sorry.

2668
01:33:18,500 --> 01:33:19,733
Odessa steps sequence?

2669
01:33:19,767 --> 01:33:21,067
I don't think I know that one.

2670
01:33:21,100 --> 01:33:22,300
No.

2671
01:33:22,333 --> 01:33:25,167
And yet I'm intimately familiar,
with a frame-by-frame basis,

2672
01:33:25,200 --> 01:33:27,933
with a major part of one
Robert L. Lippert's output.

2673
01:33:27,967 --> 01:33:28,900
Why?

2674
01:33:28,933 --> 01:33:29,733
Why?
Why?

2675
01:33:29,767 --> 01:33:30,333
I don't understand it.

2676
01:33:30,367 --> 01:33:30,933
I--

2677
01:33:30,967 --> 01:33:31,400
-Crow.

2678
01:33:31,433 --> 01:33:31,900
Crow.

2679
01:33:31,933 --> 01:33:33,767
Hey, take it easy.

2680
01:33:33,800 --> 01:33:34,400
There's no need to--

2681
01:33:34,433 --> 01:33:35,600
-Hey, Crow's right, Joel.

2682
01:33:35,633 --> 01:33:38,700
You know, there's only so
much that we can take, really.

2683
01:33:38,733 --> 01:33:40,367
-Well, the fact
is, you guys might

2684
01:33:40,400 --> 01:33:43,333
be surprised to know that Robert
Lippert was quite an innovator

2685
01:33:43,333 --> 01:33:44,233
in his time.

2686
01:33:44,267 --> 01:33:45,367
-Oh, really?

2687
01:33:45,433 --> 01:33:47,233
-In fact, I've prepared a
special presentation using

2688
01:33:47,267 --> 01:33:47,667
our--

2689
01:33:47,700 --> 01:33:48,867
-No, no, no, no.

2690
01:33:48,900 --> 01:33:49,433
No you don't.

2691
01:33:49,467 --> 01:33:51,800
-No, no, no, no, no, no.

2692
01:33:51,833 --> 01:33:52,767
-Oh, You don't want to see that?

2693
01:33:52,800 --> 01:33:55,333
Well, fortunately Cambot
and I have worked up

2694
01:33:55,367 --> 01:33:57,267
a little sound and light show.

2695
01:33:57,300 --> 01:33:59,033
Cambot, cue that up, please.

2696
01:33:59,067 --> 01:33:59,900
-No.

2697
01:33:59,933 --> 01:34:00,833
Stop the music.

2698
01:34:00,867 --> 01:34:03,933
Cue that light, Cambot,
or I'll kill you.

2699
01:34:03,967 --> 01:34:05,833
-That's better.

2700
01:34:05,867 --> 01:34:07,833
-Well, I don't see
why it would bug you

2701
01:34:07,867 --> 01:34:08,967
that I would try
to do such a thing.

2702
01:34:09,000 --> 01:34:11,567
-Because those artist's
renderings, musical tributes,

2703
01:34:11,600 --> 01:34:12,333
I don't think so, Joel.

2704
01:34:12,367 --> 01:34:13,133
Come on.

2705
01:34:13,167 --> 01:34:15,000
-I'm just trying
to show you that--

2706
01:34:15,033 --> 01:34:16,933
-Joel, Lippert is not worth it.

2707
01:34:16,933 --> 01:34:18,300
No, no.

2708
01:34:18,333 --> 01:34:20,467
-Well, there's just
one thing that he

2709
01:34:20,500 --> 01:34:21,667
did that I would
like to show you,

2710
01:34:21,700 --> 01:34:23,933
because I spent a lot of time
in the shop working on it.

2711
01:34:23,967 --> 01:34:24,933
Here it is.

2712
01:34:24,967 --> 01:34:25,867
-Show us your little toy.

2713
01:34:25,900 --> 01:34:27,800
-Robert Lippert was
the first filmmaker

2714
01:34:27,833 --> 01:34:29,967
to ever use a thing
called a theremin.

2715
01:34:30,000 --> 01:34:33,900
And a theremin creates
a body of particles.

2716
01:34:33,933 --> 01:34:35,133
And when you interrupt
the particles,

2717
01:34:35,167 --> 01:34:36,200
it changes the sound.

2718
01:34:36,233 --> 01:34:38,367
You may have heard it in
other space movies before.

2719
01:34:38,400 --> 01:34:39,067
-Sure.

2720
01:34:39,100 --> 01:34:39,967
-Here, let's turn it on here.

2721
01:34:40,000 --> 01:34:40,867
Let's try it.

2722
01:34:40,900 --> 01:34:42,800
-Oh, that's rather
pleasing, isn't it?

2723
01:34:42,833 --> 01:34:44,033
-Yeah.

2724
01:34:44,067 --> 01:34:44,567
-Neat.

2725
01:34:44,600 --> 01:34:45,467
-Whoa.

2726
01:34:45,500 --> 01:34:47,300
That's interesting
the way it changes--

2727
01:34:47,333 --> 01:34:48,167
-Sounds like--

2728
01:34:48,200 --> 01:34:49,533
-Well, thank you
very much for sharing

2729
01:34:49,567 --> 01:34:50,833
your little toy with us, Joel.

2730
01:34:50,867 --> 01:34:52,133
You can turn it
off any time, now.

2731
01:34:52,167 --> 01:34:52,800
-Thank you.

2732
01:34:52,833 --> 01:34:53,433
-It starts to get a little--

2733
01:34:53,467 --> 01:34:55,200
-Thank you, Robert Klein.

2734
01:34:55,233 --> 01:34:56,033
Thank you.

2735
01:34:56,067 --> 01:34:57,300
-Thanks a lot.

2736
01:34:57,333 --> 01:34:59,833
OK, so why don't you take that
down to your cabin, there Joel?

2737
01:35:02,300 --> 01:35:03,267
Joel?

2738
01:35:03,300 --> 01:35:05,233
I guess it's up to us to
take care of business.

2739
01:35:05,267 --> 01:35:05,633
-Guess so.

2740
01:35:05,667 --> 01:35:06,433
[Crashing]

2741
01:35:06,467 --> 01:35:08,900
-Oh, just set that
anywhere, Joel.

2742
01:35:08,933 --> 01:35:09,900
Hope he didn't break it.

2743
01:35:09,933 --> 01:35:12,267
Well, so let's read this
letter that's down here.

2744
01:35:12,300 --> 01:35:13,367
-Yep, go ahead.

2745
01:35:13,400 --> 01:35:15,933
-Uh, after he leaves.

2746
01:35:15,967 --> 01:35:19,300
Joel, could you please--
so it says here--

2747
01:35:19,333 --> 01:35:20,000
[crashing]

2748
01:35:20,033 --> 01:35:21,400
-Joel, if it bends it's funny.

2749
01:35:21,433 --> 01:35:23,233
If it breaks, it's not funny.

2750
01:35:23,267 --> 01:35:24,133
-Yikes.

2751
01:35:24,167 --> 01:35:26,167
OK, it says, dear
sirs, it's us again,

2752
01:35:26,200 --> 01:35:28,967
the Mistys from Milwaukee,
with our weekly approbations

2753
01:35:29,000 --> 01:35:29,867
of doom.

2754
01:35:29,900 --> 01:35:32,600
As usual, we-- uh,
thank you, Joel-- we

2755
01:35:32,633 --> 01:35:33,667
had a great time
with the latest show.

2756
01:35:33,700 --> 01:35:35,967
We wanted to point out a
few things regarding the--

2757
01:35:36,000 --> 01:35:36,667
[crashing]

2758
01:35:36,700 --> 01:35:37,700
-Thank you again, Joel.

2759
01:35:37,733 --> 01:35:40,067
Enclosed-- hey, they
enclosed some scorecards.

2760
01:35:40,100 --> 01:35:40,767
Hey Cambot, put these up.

2761
01:35:40,800 --> 01:35:41,867
These are really interesting.

2762
01:35:41,900 --> 01:35:44,967
Now look, they actually rate
us on a scale of 1 to 10

2763
01:35:45,000 --> 01:35:49,167
on the movie, on sarcasm,
on the invention exchange,

2764
01:35:49,200 --> 01:35:50,400
and these host segments.

2765
01:35:50,433 --> 01:35:52,100
What are host segments?

2766
01:35:52,133 --> 01:35:53,167
-Uh, I don't know.

2767
01:35:53,200 --> 01:35:54,100
-Got me on that one.

2768
01:35:54,133 --> 01:35:55,433
OK, now what are we
supposed to do now?

2769
01:35:55,467 --> 01:35:58,133
-Uh, say something
to the scientists.

2770
01:35:58,133 --> 01:35:58,767
-Oh.

2771
01:35:58,833 --> 01:36:00,067
Hey guys, are you watching?

2772
01:36:00,067 --> 01:36:01,000
How did it go?

2773
01:36:01,033 --> 01:36:02,000
What do you think?

2774
01:36:02,033 --> 01:36:05,167
-I think we should go
to the Kurosawa Festival

2775
01:36:05,200 --> 01:36:06,367
at the Geripera Cinema.

2776
01:36:06,400 --> 01:36:07,400
What do you think, Frank?

2777
01:36:07,433 --> 01:36:08,400
-Oh, forget that.

2778
01:36:08,433 --> 01:36:10,100
What about the theremin?

2779
01:36:10,133 --> 01:36:12,567
Woo--

2780
01:36:12,600 --> 01:36:14,267
-I have another idea.

2781
01:36:14,300 --> 01:36:16,200
Until next time, Joel-y.

2782
01:36:18,867 --> 01:36:19,433
-Missed.

